{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011AZDJGGxCcaYKDr383Lrst","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Paper Framing — meta_brain_lora_routing\n2\t\n3\t## Target Venue\n4\t- Primary: CoRL 2026 (Austin, Nov 9-12)\n5\t- Abstract DDL: 2026-05-26\n6\t- Paper DDL: 2026-05-29\n7\t\n8\t## Core Insight\n9\t\n10\tVLA internal representations already encode rich perturbation-type-specific information (Phase 0: single-layer F1=1.0 at K=1), yet existing robustness methods either ignore this diagnostic signal entirely (VLA-GSE, RobustVLA) or only use it for detection without correction (SAFE). Meta-Brain closes the loop: diagnose perturbation type from frozen activations, then route to a type-specialized LoRA expert for targeted correction — achieving the \"right fix for the right failure\" at 20x fewer parameters than perturbation-agnostic capacity routing.\n11\t\n12\t## Core Claims (3)\n13\t\n14\t1. **Perturbation Diagnosability** — Frozen VLA (pi0) internal activations at a single layer (PaliGemma layer 6) encode perturbation type with near-perfect accuracy (3-class macro-F1 >= 0.99), enabling real-time, zero-overhead perturbation diagnosis from a single forward pass.\n15\t\n16\t2. **Targeted Correction > Uniform Correction** — Perturbation-type-specific LoRA experts (Visual/Spatial/Physical), routed by the diagnosed type, outperform a universal LoRA baseline of equivalent total parameter budget on LIBERO-Plus, demonstrating that routing by perturbation type is a more efficient use of adaptation capacity than uniform correction.\n17\t\n18\t3. **Parameter-Efficient Robustness** — Meta-Brain (~6M trainable params: 3x rank-16 LoRA + PAR router) achieves competitive robustness gain-over-frozen-baseline compared to VLA-GSE (114M params), establishing perturbation-diagnostic routing as a parameter-efficient alternative to capacity-based expert routing.\n19\t\n20\t## Story Spine\n21\t\n22\t- **Problem**: VLAs achieve >90% on standard LIBERO but collapse under perturbation (pi0: 53.6% on LIBERO-Plus). Different perturbation types disrupt different processing stages — visual perturbations corrupt early representations, spatial perturbations invalidate action trajectories, physical perturbations break force assumptions. Yet all existing robustness methods apply either single-modality correction or perturbation-agnostic uniform adaptation.\n23\t\n24\t- **Existing approaches & gaps**: VLA-GSE (114M params, 81.2% on LIBERO-Plus) demonstrates routing helps, but routes by per-token capacity needs — the same gating configuration handles camera (64.4%) and layout (82.6%) without perturbation awareness. FTM achieves 90% on visual perturbations with 4K params but cannot handle spatial/physical. RobustVLA adds +12.6% via uniform adversarial training. No method combines perturbation diagnosis with type-specific correction.\n25\t\n26\t- **Our framing**: We propose perturbation-diagnostic routing as a new design dimension for VLA robustness, orthogonal to capacity-based routing (VLA-GSE), rank-based adaptation (LoRA-SP), and task-based routing (MoIRA/CORAL). We show that VLA internals already \"know\" what went wrong — they just lack the mechanism to apply the right fix. Meta-Brain adds that mechanism with minimal parameters.\n27\t\n28\t- **Headline result template**: \"On LIBERO-Plus, Meta-Brain achieves X% with 6M trainable parameters, improving Y% over frozen pi0 baseline and Z% over a parameter-matched universal LoRA, while VLA-GSE requires 114M parameters to achieve 81.2%.\"\n29\t\n30\t## Contribution Structure\n31\t\n32\t1. **Empirical finding**: VLA internal representations encode perturbation type at single-layer, single-step granularity — enabling real-time diagnosis without external modules or multi-step observation buffers\n33\t2. **Architecture**: Meta-Brain — a modular, parameter-efficient framework (PAR router + LoRA bank + optional Correction Memory) that routes corrective LoRA experts by diagnosed perturbation type\n34\t3. **Design dimension analysis**: Systematic comparison of perturbation-diagnostic routing vs. capacity-based routing vs. universal adaptation, establishing perturbation-awareness as an orthogonal and composable design axis\n35\t\n36\t## Venue Fit (CoRL 2026)\n37\t\n38\t**Strengths**:\n39\t- Pi0 backbone + LIBERO-Plus eval is core CoRL territory\n40\t- Architecture operates on representations (sim evaluation methodologically sufficient)\n41\t- Parameter efficiency matters for real-robot deployment (6M vs 114M)\n42\t- Single forward pass routing = no latency overhead for real-time control\n43\t\n44\t**Risks**:\n45\t- Sim-only evaluation (mitigated: LIBERO-Plus is CVPR 2026 standard, sim-only is accepted pattern at CoRL for architecture contributions)\n46\t- Single backbone (pi0) — need gain-over-baseline framing to enable contextualized comparison with VLA-GSE (Qwen3-VL-4B)\n47\t- Absolute performance may trail SRPO (82.1%) / CorridorVLA (83.21%) which use full training — frame as parameter-efficient approach, not absolute SOTA chase\n48\t\n49\t## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns\n50\t\n51\t### C1: \"Phase 0 classification is too easy — trivially solvable routing\"\n52\t**Risk**: High Phase 0 accuracy (F1=0.993) may suggest the task is trivial and routing adds no value.\n53\t**Response**: Easy diagnosis ≠ easy correction. The value of routing is in Phase 1 (specialized vs universal LoRA), not Phase 0. Easy routing is actually desirable — it means the router adds zero overhead concern. Ablation exp-005 directly tests whether routing matters.\n54\t\n55\t### C2: \"Sim-only evaluation, no real robot\"\n56\t**Risk**: CoRL values real-world experiments.\n57\t**Response**: LIBERO-Plus is the standard robustness benchmark (CVPR 2026). Meta-Brain operates on representations, not hardware-specific signals. Sim-only is the established evaluation pattern for VLA architecture contributions at CoRL (SAFE, VLA-GSE, RobustVLA all sim-only on LIBERO variants).\n58\t\n59\t### C3: \"Single backbone (pi0), limited generalizability\"\n60\t**Risk**: Results may be specific to pi0's PaliGemma architecture.\n61\t**Response**: (a) Phase 0 finding (perturbation classifiability) is grounded in mechanistic evidence across multiple VLAs (SAFE on OpenVLA, probing on OpenVLA, feature steering on pi0.5 + OpenVLA). (b) Tier 3 cross-backbone experiment on SmolVLA/OpenVLA if time permits. (c) Gain-over-baseline metric enables fair comparison across backbones.\n62\t\n63\t### C4: \"Parameter budget comparison unfair — different backbone from VLA-GSE\"\n64\t**Risk**: Comparing 6M (on pi0) vs 114M (on Qwen3-VL-4B) is apples-to-oranges.\n65\t**Response**: Compare gain-over-respective-LoRA-baseline: VLA-GSE gains +12.0% over LoRA (69.2% → 81.2%); Meta-Brain should gain X% over universal LoRA on pi0. Parameter efficiency = gain per trainable parameter. Also report absolute numbers with caveat.\n66\t\n67\t### C5: \"3-class taxonomy may be too coarse\"\n68\t**Risk**: Lumping camera+light+background+noise into \"Visual\" may lose useful distinctions.\n69\t**Response**: Phase 0 6-class analysis shows intra-Visual perturbations are more similar to each other than to Spatial/Physical. 3-class matches the mechanistic distinction in VLA processing (early/mid/late layers). Ablation: 6-class routing vs 3-class routing comparison.\n70\t\n71\t### C6: \"Correction Memory adds complexity for marginal gain\"\n72\t**Risk**: CM may seem over-engineered for routing-weight modulation.\n73\t**Response**: CM is presented as an optional module. Ablation (with/without CM) quantifies its contribution. CM adds only 1 learned parameter (alpha) + CPU-bound FAISS.\n74\t\n75\t## Baselines & Comparisons\n76\t\n77\t| Method | Type | Params | Expected Comparison |\n78\t|--------|------|--------|-------------------|\n79\t| Frozen pi0 | Lower bound | 0 | Our improvement over this = headline number |\n80\t| Universal LoRA (rank 16) | Ablation | ~2M | Same total capacity, no routing — tests Claim 2 |\n81\t| Meta-Brain (PAR + ALB) | Ours | ~6M | Full system |\n82\t| Meta-Brain + CM | Ours (full) | ~6M + 1 | With Correction Memory |\n83\t| Oracle routing | Upper bound | ~6M | Perfect routing — gap to Meta-Brain = routing quality |\n84\t| FTM/FLA | Visual-only | 4K-4.7M | Strong on visual, cannot do spatial/physical |\n85\t| VLA-GSE | Capacity routing | 114M | Different backbone (Qwen3-VL-4B), compare gain-over-LoRA |\n86\t| RobustVLA | Adversarial training | Full model | Uniform approach, compare robustness gain |\n87\t\n88\t## Key Figures (Planned)\n89\t\n90\t1. **Architecture diagram**: Frozen pi0 → PAR (layer 6) → soft routing → LoRA-V/S/P bank → corrected output\n91\t2. **Phase 0 evidence**: t-SNE of layer 6 activations colored by perturbation type + confusion matrix\n92\t3. **Main result table**: LIBERO-Plus per-perturbation breakdown (7 categories × methods)\n93\t4. **Ablation ladder**: Bar chart — Frozen → Universal LoRA → Meta-Brain → Oracle\n94\t5. **Parameter efficiency**: Scatter plot — gain-over-baseline vs trainable parameters (Meta-Brain vs VLA-GSE vs FTM vs RobustVLA)\n95\t6. **Routing analysis**: Heatmap of PAR soft routing weights per perturbation type (should be near-diagonal)\n96\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f10e81d9-0851-41df-87e8-bf73f3c49003","uuid":"6715dab8-bfb7-4c17-b625-999447821b9d","timestamp":"2026-05-15T16:18:44.940Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-be-idea-gen-0515/projects/meta_brain_lora_routing/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260515T154653/median/paper_framing.md","content":"# Paper Framing — meta_brain_lora_routing\n\n## Target Venue\n- Primary: CoRL 2026 (Austin, Nov 9-12)\n- Abstract DDL: 2026-05-26\n- Paper DDL: 2026-05-29\n\n## Core Insight\n\nVLA internal representations already encode rich perturbation-type-specific information (Phase 0: single-layer F1=1.0 at K=1), yet existing robustness methods either ignore this diagnostic signal entirely (VLA-GSE, RobustVLA) or only use it for detection without correction (SAFE). Meta-Brain closes the loop: diagnose perturbation type from frozen activations, then route to a type-specialized LoRA expert for targeted correction — achieving the \"right fix for the right failure\" at 20x fewer parameters than perturbation-agnostic capacity routing.\n\n## Core Claims (3)\n\n1. **Perturbation Diagnosability** — Frozen VLA (pi0) internal activations at a single layer (PaliGemma layer 6) encode perturbation type with near-perfect accuracy (3-class macro-F1 >= 0.99), enabling real-time, zero-overhead perturbation diagnosis from a single forward pass.\n\n2. **Targeted Correction > Uniform Correction** — Perturbation-type-specific LoRA experts (Visual/Spatial/Physical), routed by the diagnosed type, outperform a universal LoRA baseline of equivalent total parameter budget on LIBERO-Plus, demonstrating that routing by perturbation type is a more efficient use of adaptation capacity than uniform correction.\n\n3. **Parameter-Efficient Robustness** — Meta-Brain (~6M trainable params: 3x rank-16 LoRA + PAR router) achieves competitive robustness gain-over-frozen-baseline compared to VLA-GSE (114M params), establishing perturbation-diagnostic routing as a parameter-efficient alternative to capacity-based expert routing.\n\n## Story Spine\n\n- **Problem**: VLAs achieve >90% on standard LIBERO but collapse under perturbation (pi0: 53.6% on LIBERO-Plus). Different perturbation types disrupt different processing stages — visual perturbations corrupt early representations, spatial perturbations invalidate action trajectories, physical perturbations break force assumptions. Yet all existing robustness methods apply either single-modality correction or perturbation-agnostic uniform adaptation.\n\n- **Existing approaches & gaps**: VLA-GSE (114M params, 81.2% on LIBERO-Plus) demonstrates routing helps, but routes by per-token capacity needs — the same gating configuration handles camera (64.4%) and layout (82.6%) without perturbation awareness. FTM achieves 90% on visual perturbations with 4K params but cannot handle spatial/physical. RobustVLA adds +12.6% via uniform adversarial training. No method combines perturbation diagnosis with type-specific correction.\n\n- **Our framing**: We propose perturbation-diagnostic routing as a new design dimension for VLA robustness, orthogonal to capacity-based routing (VLA-GSE), rank-based adaptation (LoRA-SP), and task-based routing (MoIRA/CORAL). We show that VLA internals already \"know\" what went wrong — they just lack the mechanism to apply the right fix. Meta-Brain adds that mechanism with minimal parameters.\n\n- **Headline result template**: \"On LIBERO-Plus, Meta-Brain achieves X% with 6M trainable parameters, improving Y% over frozen pi0 baseline and Z% over a parameter-matched universal LoRA, while VLA-GSE requires 114M parameters to achieve 81.2%.\"\n\n## Contribution Structure\n\n1. **Empirical finding**: VLA internal representations encode perturbation type at single-layer, single-step granularity — enabling real-time diagnosis without external modules or multi-step observation buffers\n2. **Architecture**: Meta-Brain — a modular, parameter-efficient framework (PAR router + LoRA bank + optional Correction Memory) that routes corrective LoRA experts by diagnosed perturbation type\n3. **Design dimension analysis**: Systematic comparison of perturbation-diagnostic routing vs. capacity-based routing vs. universal adaptation, establishing perturbation-awareness as an orthogonal and composable design axis\n\n## Venue Fit (CoRL 2026)\n\n**Strengths**:\n- Pi0 backbone + LIBERO-Plus eval is core CoRL territory\n- Architecture operates on representations (sim evaluation methodologically sufficient)\n- Parameter efficiency matters for real-robot deployment (6M vs 114M)\n- Single forward pass routing = no latency overhead for real-time control\n\n**Risks**:\n- Sim-only evaluation (mitigated: LIBERO-Plus is CVPR 2026 standard, sim-only is accepted pattern at CoRL for architecture contributions)\n- Single backbone (pi0) — need gain-over-baseline framing to enable contextualized comparison with VLA-GSE (Qwen3-VL-4B)\n- Absolute performance may trail SRPO (82.1%) / CorridorVLA (83.21%) which use full training — frame as parameter-efficient approach, not absolute SOTA chase\n\n## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns\n\n### C1: \"Phase 0 classification is too easy — trivially solvable routing\"\n**Risk**: High Phase 0 accuracy (F1=0.993) may suggest the task is trivial and routing adds no value.\n**Response**: Easy diagnosis ≠ easy correction. The value of routing is in Phase 1 (specialized vs universal LoRA), not Phase 0. Easy routing is actually desirable — it means the router adds zero overhead concern. Ablation exp-005 directly tests whether routing matters.\n\n### C2: \"Sim-only evaluation, no real robot\"\n**Risk**: CoRL values real-world experiments.\n**Response**: LIBERO-Plus is the standard robustness benchmark (CVPR 2026). Meta-Brain operates on representations, not hardware-specific signals. Sim-only is the established evaluation pattern for VLA architecture contributions at CoRL (SAFE, VLA-GSE, RobustVLA all sim-only on LIBERO variants).\n\n### C3: \"Single backbone (pi0), limited generalizability\"\n**Risk**: Results may be specific to pi0's PaliGemma architecture.\n**Response**: (a) Phase 0 finding (perturbation classifiability) is grounded in mechanistic evidence across multiple VLAs (SAFE on OpenVLA, probing on OpenVLA, feature steering on pi0.5 + OpenVLA). (b) Tier 3 cross-backbone experiment on SmolVLA/OpenVLA if time permits. (c) Gain-over-baseline metric enables fair comparison across backbones.\n\n### C4: \"Parameter budget comparison unfair — different backbone from VLA-GSE\"\n**Risk**: Comparing 6M (on pi0) vs 114M (on Qwen3-VL-4B) is apples-to-oranges.\n**Response**: Compare gain-over-respective-LoRA-baseline: VLA-GSE gains +12.0% over LoRA (69.2% → 81.2%); Meta-Brain should gain X% over universal LoRA on pi0. Parameter efficiency = gain per trainable parameter. Also report absolute numbers with caveat.\n\n### C5: \"3-class taxonomy may be too coarse\"\n**Risk**: Lumping camera+light+background+noise into \"Visual\" may lose useful distinctions.\n**Response**: Phase 0 6-class analysis shows intra-Visual perturbations are more similar to each other than to Spatial/Physical. 3-class matches the mechanistic distinction in VLA processing (early/mid/late layers). Ablation: 6-class routing vs 3-class routing comparison.\n\n### C6: \"Correction Memory adds complexity for marginal gain\"\n**Risk**: CM may seem over-engineered for routing-weight modulation.\n**Response**: CM is presented as an optional module. Ablation (with/without CM) quantifies its contribution. CM adds only 1 learned parameter (alpha) + CPU-bound FAISS.\n\n## Baselines & Comparisons\n\n| Method | Type | Params | Expected Comparison |\n|--------|------|--------|-------------------|\n| Frozen pi0 | Lower bound | 0 | Our improvement over this = headline number |\n| Universal LoRA (rank 16) | Ablation | ~2M | Same total capacity, no routing — tests Claim 2 |\n| Meta-Brain (PAR + ALB) | Ours | ~6M | Full system |\n| Meta-Brain + CM | Ours (full) | ~6M + 1 | With Correction Memory |\n| Oracle routing | Upper bound | ~6M | Perfect routing — gap to Meta-Brain = routing quality |\n| FTM/FLA | Visual-only | 4K-4.7M | Strong on visual, cannot do spatial/physical |\n| VLA-GSE | Capacity routing | 114M | Different backbone (Qwen3-VL-4B), compare gain-over-LoRA |\n| RobustVLA | Adversarial training | Full model | Uniform approach, compare robustness gain |\n\n## Key Figures (Planned)\n\n1. **Architecture diagram**: Frozen pi0 → PAR (layer 6) → soft routing → LoRA-V/S/P bank → corrected output\n2. **Phase 0 evidence**: t-SNE of layer 6 activations colored by perturbation type + confusion matrix\n3. **Main result table**: LIBERO-Plus per-perturbation breakdown (7 categories × methods)\n4. **Ablation ladder**: Bar chart — Frozen → Universal LoRA → Meta-Brain → Oracle\n5. **Parameter efficiency**: Scatter plot — gain-over-baseline vs trainable parameters (Meta-Brain vs VLA-GSE vs FTM vs RobustVLA)\n6. **Routing analysis**: Heatmap of PAR soft routing weights per perturbation type (should be near-diagonal)\n","numLines":96,"startLine":1,"totalLines":96}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016GbXKco2b9mh5oxaxN2LWT","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t[\n2\t  {\n3\t    \"name\": \"LoRA Expert Training — Specialized vs Universal\",\n4\t    \"description\": \"Train 4 rank-16 LoRA adapters on frozen pi0: LoRA-V (visual data), LoRA-S (spatial data), LoRA-P (physical data), Universal (mixed all-type data). Evaluate each on all 7 perturbation types to obtain the cross-type performance matrix.\\n\",\n5\t    \"results\": {\n6\t      \"lora_v_visual_avg\": 78.3,\n7\t      \"lora_v_camera\": 75.2,\n8\t      \"lora_v_light\": 82.0,\n9\t      \"lora_v_background\": 78.4,\n10\t      \"lora_v_noise\": 77.6,\n11\t      \"lora_v_spatial_avg\": 54.0,\n12\t      \"lora_v_physical\": 50.6,\n13\t      \"lora_s_spatial_avg\": 68.0,\n14\t      \"lora_s_distractor\": 69.4,\n15\t      \"lora_s_layout\": 66.6,\n16\t      \"lora_s_visual_avg\": 46.9,\n17\t      \"lora_s_physical\": 49.2,\n18\t      \"lora_p_physical\": 78.0,\n19\t      \"lora_p_visual_avg\": 47.9,\n20\t      \"lora_p_spatial_avg\": 53.7,\n21\t      \"universal_overall\": 65.4,\n22\t      \"universal_visual_avg\": 65.35,\n23\t      \"universal_spatial_avg\": 63.7,\n24\t      \"universal_physical\": 69.0,\n25\t      \"specialization_delta_visual\": 12.95,\n26\t      \"specialization_delta_spatial\": 4.3,\n27\t      \"specialization_delta_physical\": 9.0,\n28\t      \"specialization_delta_max\": 12.95\n29\t    },\n30\t    \"tables\": [\n31\t      {\n32\t        \"name\": \"Cross-Type Expert Performance Matrix (success rate %)\",\n33\t        \"header\": [\n34\t          \"Method\",\n35\t          \"camera\",\n36\t          \"light\",\n37\t          \"background\",\n38\t          \"noise\",\n39\t          \"distractor\",\n40\t          \"layout\",\n41\t          \"robot\",\n42\t          \"Overall\"\n43\t        ],\n44\t        \"rows\": [\n45\t          [\n46\t            \"Frozen pi0\",\n47\t            47.6,\n48\t            56.2,\n49\t            51.8,\n50\t            49.4,\n51\t            58.6,\n52\t            57.8,\n53\t            53.8,\n54\t            53.6\n55\t          ],\n56\t          [\n57\t            \"LoRA-V\",\n58\t            75.2,\n59\t            82.0,\n60\t            78.4,\n61\t            77.6,\n62\t            54.2,\n63\t            53.8,\n64\t            50.6,\n65\t            67.4\n66\t          ],\n67\t          [\n68\t            \"LoRA-S\",\n69\t            44.2,\n70\t            50.4,\n71\t            47.8,\n72\t            45.0,\n73\t            69.4,\n74\t            66.6,\n75\t            49.2,\n76\t            53.2\n77\t          ],\n78\t          [\n79\t            \"LoRA-P\",\n80\t            45.6,\n81\t            51.2,\n82\t            48.4,\n83\t            46.2,\n84\t            54.0,\n85\t            53.4,\n86\t            78.0,\n87\t            53.8\n88\t          ],\n89\t          [\n90\t            \"Universal\",\n91\t            62.8,\n92\t            69.4,\n93\t            66.2,\n94\t            63.0,\n95\t            64.8,\n96\t            62.6,\n97\t            69.0,\n98\t            65.4\n99\t          ]\n100\t        ]\n101\t      },\n102\t      {\n103\t        \"name\": \"Specialization Delta (expert own-type minus universal same-type)\",\n104\t        \"header\": [\n105\t          \"Coarse Type\",\n106\t          \"Expert Own-Type\",\n107\t          \"Universal Same-Type\",\n108\t          \"Delta\"\n109\t        ],\n110\t        \"rows\": [\n111\t          [\n112\t            \"Visual\",\n113\t            78.3,\n114\t            65.35,\n115\t            12.95\n116\t          ],\n117\t          [\n118\t            \"Spatial\",\n119\t            68.0,\n120\t            63.7,\n121\t            4.3\n122\t          ],\n123\t          [\n124\t            \"Physical\",\n125\t            78.0,\n126\t            69.0,\n127\t            9.0\n128\t          ]\n129\t        ]\n130\t      }\n131\t    ],\n132\t    \"figures\": [],\n133\t    \"analysis\": \"The cross-type matrix is the foundational evidence for why routing matters: correct expert helps significantly (+9 to +13% over universal), wrong expert hurts severely (below frozen). This asymmetry means random or poor routing can be worse than no routing at all, motivating the need for accurate PAR classification. The weak spatial specialization is interpretable: layout and distractor perturbations change the goal-relevant spatial configuration, requiring trajectory-level replanning that a single-step representation correction cannot provide. This is a fundamental limitation of the LoRA-based approach for spatial perturbations, not a routing failure. The universal LoRA's surprising strength (65.4%) sets a high bar for Meta-Brain — the routing system must do substantially better to justify 3× the parameters.\\n\\n\\nVisual expert (LoRA-V) shows the strongest specialization (+13% over universal on visual types) because visual perturbations (camera/light/background/noise) corrupt early PaliGemma representations in a consistent, localized manner — rank-16 LoRA can learn a compact correction to these early-layer distortions. Physical expert (LoRA-P) shows strong but smaller specialization (+9%) because robot perturbations (changed dynamics/mass) affect action-space outputs which are more distributed across layers. Spatial expert (LoRA-S) shows weak specialization (+4.3%) because layout and distractor perturbations fundamentally change the required trajectory — not just a representational shift but a different action sequence — and single-step LoRA correction at the representation level cannot replan a trajectory. Cross-type performance degrades sharply (below frozen baseline) because each specialized expert learns type-specific correction biases that actively harm representations when applied to the wrong perturbation type. Universal LoRA at 65.4% is stronger than expected because mixed training provides partial cross-type robustness — the LoRA learns an average correction that modestly helps all types without hurting any.\\n\"\n134\t  },\n135\t  {\n136\t    \"name\": \"Meta-Brain Assembly — PAR Router + LoRA Bank\",\n137\t    \"description\": \"Assemble PAR router (PaliGemma layer 6, 2-layer MLP, initialized from Phase 0 weights) with 3 LoRA experts. Soft routing: h_corrected = h_frozen + Σ_k p(type_k) · LoRA_k(h_frozen). End-to-end routing fine-tuning with frozen LoRA parameters (only tune PAR weights). Evaluate on full LIBERO-Plus benchmark.\\n\",\n138\t    \"results\": {\n139\t      \"libero_plus_overall\": 71.4,\n140\t      \"per_type_camera\": 71.0,\n141\t      \"per_type_light\": 77.6,\n142\t      \"per_type_background\": 74.4,\n143\t      \"per_type_noise\": 73.0,\n144\t      \"per_type_distractor\": 65.2,\n145\t      \"per_type_layout\": 64.4,\n146\t      \"per_type_robot\": 74.2,\n147\t      \"visual_avg\": 74.0,\n148\t      \"spatial_avg\": 64.8,\n149\t      \"physical\": 74.2,\n150\t      \"gain_over_frozen\": 17.8,\n151\t      \"gain_over_universal\": 6.0,\n152\t      \"routing_diag_visual\": 0.82,\n153\t      \"routing_diag_spatial\": 0.68,\n154\t      \"routing_diag_physical\": 0.8,\n155\t      \"routing_diagonality_mean\": 0.77,\n156\t      \"hard_assignment_accuracy\": 89.0\n157\t    },\n158\t    \"tables\": [\n159\t      {\n160\t        \"name\": \"Meta-Brain Per-Perturbation Success Rate (%)\",\n161\t        \"header\": [\n162\t          \"Perturbation\",\n163\t          \"Frozen\",\n164\t          \"Universal\",\n165\t          \"Meta-Brain\",\n166\t          \"Gain over Universal\"\n167\t        ],\n168\t        \"rows\": [\n169\t          [\n170\t            \"camera\",\n171\t            47.6,\n172\t            62.8,\n173\t            71.0,\n174\t            8.2\n175\t          ],\n176\t          [\n177\t            \"light\",\n178\t            56.2,\n179\t            69.4,\n180\t            77.6,\n181\t            8.2\n182\t          ],\n183\t          [\n184\t            \"background\",\n185\t            51.8,\n186\t            66.2,\n187\t            74.4,\n188\t            8.2\n189\t          ],\n190\t          [\n191\t            \"noise\",\n192\t            49.4,\n193\t            63.0,\n194\t            73.0,\n195\t            10.0\n196\t          ],\n197\t          [\n198\t            \"distractor\",\n199\t            58.6,\n200\t            64.8,\n201\t            65.2,\n202\t            0.4\n203\t          ],\n204\t          [\n205\t            \"layout\",\n206\t            57.8,\n207\t            62.6,\n208\t            64.4,\n209\t            1.8\n210\t          ],\n211\t          [\n212\t            \"robot\",\n213\t            53.8,\n214\t            69.0,\n215\t            74.2,\n216\t            5.2\n217\t          ],\n218\t          [\n219\t            \"Overall\",\n220\t            53.6,\n221\t            65.4,\n222\t            71.4,\n223\t            6.0\n224\t          ]\n225\t        ]\n226\t      },\n227\t      {\n228\t        \"name\": \"PAR Soft Routing Weight Matrix (mean weights per true type)\",\n229\t        \"header\": [\n230\t          \"True Type\",\n231\t          \"P(LoRA-V)\",\n232\t          \"P(LoRA-S)\",\n233\t          \"P(LoRA-P)\"\n234\t        ],\n235\t        \"rows\": [\n236\t          [\n237\t            \"Visual\",\n238\t            0.82,\n239\t            0.06,\n240\t            0.12\n241\t          ],\n242\t          [\n243\t            \"Spatial\",\n244\t            0.2,\n245\t            0.68,\n246\t            0.12\n247\t          ],\n248\t          [\n249\t            \"Physical\",\n250\t            0.12,\n251\t            0.08,\n252\t            0.8\n253\t          ]\n254\t        ]\n255\t      }\n256\t    ],\n257\t    \"figures\": [],\n258\t    \"analysis\": \"Meta-Brain achieves the target ordering (Meta-Brain > Universal > Frozen on all 7 types) but the margin varies dramatically: +8-10% on visual types, +5% on physical, only +0.4-1.8% on spatial. This asymmetry is mechanistically interpretable: visual perturbations corrupt the representation in a way that a type-specific LoRA can precisely invert, while spatial perturbations require policy-level replanning beyond representation correction. The routing weight analysis reveals the PAR router appropriately lacks confidence on spatial inputs (diag 0.68), which limits both the benefit and the harm of routing on these types. The end-to-end routing fine-tuning shifts the router from pure classification toward task-success optimization, explaining the gap between Phase 0 accuracy (99.3%) and deployed routing accuracy (~89%).\\n\\n\\nSoft routing dilutes the correction signal compared to hard (oracle) routing, costing ~4% overall. The dilution is uniform across types (3-5% per type) because even a small weight on the wrong expert introduces harmful correction biases. Spatial routing diagonality is lowest (0.68) because layout/distractor perturbations share visual features with camera/background changes at layer 6 — the PAR router's operating point. Despite this, Meta-Brain still improves over universal on spatial (+1.1%) because even imperfect soft routing toward LoRA-S is better than no type awareness. Visual gain (+8.65% over universal) is large because high routing confidence (0.82) allows LoRA-V to dominate the correction. Physical gain (+5.2%) is moderate due to good routing (0.80) but more distributed perturbation effects. Hard assignment accuracy (89%) is lower than Phase 0 classification (99.3%) because the router is fine-tuned for task success, not classification accuracy — it learns to soften assignments when mixing provides marginal task benefit.\\n\"\n259\t  },\n260\t  {\n261\t    \"name\": \"Oracle Routing — Upper Bound for Routing Quality\",\n262\t    \"description\": \"Replace PAR soft router with ground-truth perturbation type labels (hard 1-hot routing to the correct expert). Same LoRA experts from plan_002. Measures the ceiling for routing quality and quantifies PAR's gap to perfect routing.\\n\",\n263\t    \"results\": {\n264\t      \"oracle_overall\": 75.3,\n265\t      \"oracle_camera\": 75.2,\n266\t      \"oracle_light\": 82.0,\n267\t      \"oracle_background\": 78.4,\n268\t      \"oracle_noise\": 77.6,\n269\t      \"oracle_distractor\": 69.4,\n270\t      \"oracle_layout\": 66.6,\n271\t      \"oracle_robot\": 78.0,\n272\t      \"oracle_par_gap_overall\": 3.9,\n273\t      \"oracle_par_gap_visual\": 4.3,\n274\t      \"oracle_par_gap_spatial\": 3.8,\n275\t      \"oracle_par_gap_physical\": 3.8\n276\t    },\n277\t    \"tables\": [\n278\t      {\n279\t        \"name\": \"Oracle vs PAR Per-Type Gap (%)\",\n280\t        \"header\": [\n281\t          \"Perturbation\",\n282\t          \"Oracle\",\n283\t          \"Meta-Brain (PAR)\",\n284\t          \"Gap\"\n285\t        ],\n286\t        \"rows\": [\n287\t          [\n288\t            \"camera\",\n289\t            75.2,\n290\t            71.0,\n291\t            4.2\n292\t          ],\n293\t          [\n294\t            \"light\",\n295\t            82.0,\n296\t            77.6,\n297\t            4.4\n298\t          ],\n299\t          [\n300\t            \"background\",\n301\t            78.4,\n302\t            74.4,\n303\t            4.0\n304\t          ],\n305\t          [\n306\t            \"noise\",\n307\t            77.6,\n308\t            73.0,\n309\t            4.6\n310\t          ],\n311\t          [\n312\t            \"distractor\",\n313\t            69.4,\n314\t            65.2,\n315\t            4.2\n316\t          ],\n317\t          [\n318\t            \"layout\",\n319\t            66.6,\n320\t            64.4,\n321\t            2.2\n322\t          ],\n323\t          [\n324\t            \"robot\",\n325\t            78.0,\n326\t            74.2,\n327\t            3.8\n328\t          ],\n329\t          [\n330\t            \"Overall\",\n331\t            75.3,\n332\t            71.4,\n333\t            3.9\n334\t          ]\n335\t        ]\n336\t      }\n337\t    ],\n338\t    \"figures\": [],\n339\t    \"analysis\": \"The oracle-PAR gap of 3.9% is in the moderate range — large enough to matter for absolute performance but not large enough to invalidate the routing approach. The implication for Claim 2 is important: even with perfect routing, the gain over universal LoRA would be 75.3 - 65.4 = 9.9%, only ~64% more than Meta-Brain's 6.0%. This means routing quality accounts for ~40% of the potential routing benefit left on the table. For the paper, frame this as: \\\"PAR captures ~60% of the theoretically available routing benefit, with the remaining gap attributable to soft-routing dilution and spatial ambiguity.\\\" The gap also establishes headroom for future router improvements (e.g., confidence-gated hard routing).\\n\\n\\nOracle routing eliminates soft-routing dilution entirely — each perturbation type receives 100% correction from its matched expert, with zero contamination from mismatched experts. The 3.9% overall gap from Meta-Brain to oracle quantifies the cost of learned soft routing versus perfect hard routing. This gap arises from two sources: (1) non-zero weight on wrong experts injects harmful correction biases (~60% of the gap), and (2) the router's lower confidence on spatial perturbations causes LoRA-S to be under-weighted even on true spatial inputs (~40% of the gap). The gap is moderate (not catastrophic), indicating PAR routing is functional but not near-perfect. Closing this gap further would require either better features for spatial classification at layer 6, or a hybrid routing scheme that uses hard routing when confidence is high and universal fallback when confidence is low. Note that oracle performance (75.3%) still trails VLA-GSE (81.2%) by 5.9%, confirming that the performance ceiling is set by pi0 backbone capacity and LoRA rank, not routing quality.\\n\"\n340\t  },\n341\t  {\n342\t    \"name\": \"Ablation Ladder — Frozen → Universal → Meta-Brain → Oracle\",\n343\t    \"description\": \"Systematic 5-seed comparison on full LIBERO-Plus: Frozen pi0, Universal LoRA (rank 16, ~2M), Meta-Brain (PAR + 3 LoRA, ~6M), Oracle routing (ground-truth type). Report overall and per-perturbation breakdown with statistical significance. Core evidence for Claim 2.\\n\",\n344\t    \"results\": {\n345\t      \"frozen_mean\": 53.6,\n346\t      \"frozen_std\": 1.8,\n347\t      \"universal_mean\": 65.4,\n348\t      \"universal_std\": 2.1,\n349\t      \"meta_brain_mean\": 71.4,\n350\t      \"meta_brain_std\": 2.4,\n351\t      \"oracle_mean\": 75.3,\n352\t      \"oracle_std\": 1.9,\n353\t      \"gain_frozen_to_universal\": 11.8,\n354\t      \"gain_universal_to_meta_brain\": 6.0,\n355\t      \"gain_meta_brain_to_oracle\": 3.9,\n356\t      \"pvalue_universal_gt_frozen\": 0.0003,\n357\t      \"pvalue_meta_brain_gt_universal\": 0.008,\n358\t      \"pvalue_oracle_gt_meta_brain\": 0.021,\n359\t      \"meta_brain_gain_over_frozen\": 17.8\n360\t    },\n361\t    \"tables\": [\n362\t      {\n363\t        \"name\": \"Ablation Ladder — Overall Success Rate (%, 5 seeds)\",\n364\t        \"header\": [\n365\t          \"Method\",\n366\t          \"Params\",\n367\t          \"Mean\",\n368\t          \"Std\",\n369\t          \"95% CI\",\n370\t          \"p vs. Previous\"\n371\t        ],\n372\t        \"rows\": [\n373\t          [\n374\t            \"Frozen pi0\",\n375\t            0,\n376\t            53.6,\n377\t            1.8,\n378\t            \"[51.4, 55.8]\",\n379\t            \"—\"\n380\t          ],\n381\t          [\n382\t            \"Universal LoRA\",\n383\t            \"~2M\",\n384\t            65.4,\n385\t            2.1,\n386\t            \"[62.8, 68.0]\",\n387\t            \"<0.001\"\n388\t          ],\n389\t          [\n390\t            \"Meta-Brain\",\n391\t            \"~6M\",\n392\t            71.4,\n393\t            2.4,\n394\t            \"[68.4, 74.4]\",\n395\t            \"0.008\"\n396\t          ],\n397\t          [\n398\t            \"Oracle\",\n399\t            \"~6M\",\n400\t            75.3,\n401\t            1.9,\n402\t            \"[72.9, 77.7]\",\n403\t            \"0.021\"\n404\t          ]\n405\t        ]\n406\t      },\n407\t      {\n408\t        \"name\": \"Ablation Ladder — Per-Perturbation Breakdown (%, 5-seed mean)\",\n409\t        \"header\": [\n410\t          \"Method\",\n411\t          \"camera\",\n412\t          \"light\",\n413\t          \"bg\",\n414\t          \"noise\",\n415\t          \"distr\",\n416\t          \"layout\",\n417\t          \"robot\",\n418\t          \"Overall\"\n419\t        ],\n420\t        \"rows\": [\n421\t          [\n422\t            \"Frozen\",\n423\t            47.6,\n424\t            56.2,\n425\t            51.8,\n426\t            49.4,\n427\t            58.6,\n428\t            57.8,\n429\t            53.8,\n430\t            53.6\n431\t          ],\n432\t          [\n433\t            \"Universal\",\n434\t            62.8,\n435\t            69.4,\n436\t            66.2,\n437\t            63.0,\n438\t            64.8,\n439\t            62.6,\n440\t            69.0,\n441\t            65.4\n442\t          ],\n443\t          [\n444\t            \"Meta-Brain\",\n445\t            71.0,\n446\t            77.6,\n447\t            74.4,\n448\t            73.0,\n449\t            65.2,\n450\t            64.4,\n451\t            74.2,\n452\t            71.4\n453\t          ],\n454\t          [\n455\t            \"Oracle\",\n456\t            75.2,\n457\t            82.0,\n458\t            78.4,\n459\t            77.6,\n460\t            69.4,\n461\t            66.6,\n462\t            78.0,\n463\t            75.3\n464\t          ]\n465\t        ]\n466\t      },\n467\t      {\n468\t        \"name\": \"Routing Gain Decomposition by Coarse Type\",\n469\t        \"header\": [\n470\t          \"Coarse Type\",\n471\t          \"Universal\",\n472\t          \"Meta-Brain\",\n473\t          \"Routing Gain\",\n474\t          \"% of Oracle Ceiling\"\n475\t        ],\n476\t        \"rows\": [\n477\t          [\n478\t            \"Visual (4 types)\",\n479\t            65.35,\n480\t            74.0,\n481\t            8.65,\n482\t            \"66.8%\"\n483\t          ],\n484\t          [\n485\t            \"Spatial (2 types)\",\n486\t            63.7,\n487\t            64.8,\n488\t            1.1,\n489\t            \"25.6%\"\n490\t          ],\n491\t          [\n492\t            \"Physical (1 type)\",\n493\t            69.0,\n494\t            74.2,\n495\t            5.2,\n496\t            \"57.8%\"\n497\t          ],\n498\t          [\n499\t            \"Overall\",\n500\t            65.4,\n501\t            71.4,\n502\t            6.0,\n503\t            \"60.6%\"\n504\t          ]\n505\t        ]\n506\t      }\n507\t    ],\n508\t    \"figures\": [],\n509\t    \"analysis\": \"The ablation ladder is clean and publishable. All pairwise comparisons are significant. The key narrative point is that Meta-Brain captures 60.6% of the theoretically available routing benefit (oracle ceiling). The per-type decomposition reveals that routing works best where perturbations are localized (visual: 66.8% of ceiling captured) and least where perturbations are distributed (spatial: 25.6%). This naturally leads to the mechanistic analysis section in the paper explaining which processing stages each perturbation type disrupts. For Claim 2, the headline is: \\\"Routing by perturbation type improves over universal LoRA by 6.0 ± 2.3% (p = 0.008), with gains concentrated on perturbation types that disrupt localized processing stages.\\\" This reframing adds nuance rather than claiming universal superiority.\\n\\n\\nThe ablation ladder shows a clean monotonic ordering (Frozen < Universal < Meta-Brain < Oracle) with all adjacent pairs statistically significant at p < 0.05. Step sizes decrease going up the ladder: +11.8 (baseline adaptation), +6.0 (routing benefit), +3.9 (routing quality gap). The largest step is Frozen → Universal, confirming that simply adding LoRA adaptation captures most of the low-hanging fruit. The Universal → Meta-Brain step (+6.0%) is the headline number for Claim 2 — routing by perturbation type provides meaningful additional benefit beyond uniform adaptation, but the advantage is moderate (not dominant). The diminishing step sizes reflect a fundamental pattern: each successive refinement addresses a smaller remaining error surface. Standard deviations are 1.8-2.4%, typical for LIBERO-Plus with per-seed task ordering variance. Meta-Brain's higher std (2.4%) reflects routing sensitivity to seed-specific perturbation distributions.\\n\"\n510\t  },\n511\t  {\n512\t    \"name\": \"FTM/FLA Reproduction — Visual-Only Baseline\",\n513\t    \"description\": \"Reproduce FTM (4K params) and FLA (4.7M params) on pi0 for visual perturbation types. Compare with LoRA-V and Meta-Brain to establish visual-only baselines and demonstrate Meta-Brain's multi-type advantage.\\n\",\n514\t    \"results\": {\n515\t      \"ftm_camera\": 69.8,\n516\t      \"ftm_light\": 76.4,\n517\t      \"ftm_background\": 72.6,\n518\t      \"ftm_noise\": 68.2,\n519\t      \"ftm_visual_avg\": 71.75,\n520\t      \"ftm_overall\": 65.3,\n521\t      \"fla_camera\": 73.8,\n522\t      \"fla_light\": 80.2,\n523\t      \"fla_background\": 76.8,\n524\t      \"fla_noise\": 75.4,\n525\t      \"fla_visual_avg\": 76.55,\n526\t      \"fla_overall\": 68.1,\n527\t      \"lora_v_visual_avg\": 78.3,\n528\t      \"meta_brain_visual_avg\": 74.0,\n529\t      \"meta_brain_overall\": 71.4,\n530\t      \"meta_brain_advantage_over_ftm_overall\": 6.1,\n531\t      \"meta_brain_advantage_over_fla_overall\": 3.3\n532\t    },\n533\t    \"tables\": [\n534\t      {\n535\t        \"name\": \"Visual Perturbation Methods Comparison (%)\",\n536\t        \"header\": [\n537\t          \"Method\",\n538\t          \"Params\",\n539\t          \"camera\",\n540\t          \"light\",\n541\t          \"bg\",\n542\t          \"noise\",\n543\t          \"Visual Avg\",\n544\t          \"Overall\"\n545\t        ],\n546\t        \"rows\": [\n547\t          [\n548\t            \"FTM\",\n549\t            \"4K\",\n550\t            69.8,\n551\t            76.4,\n552\t            72.6,\n553\t            68.2,\n554\t            71.75,\n555\t            65.3\n556\t          ],\n557\t          [\n558\t            \"FLA\",\n559\t            \"4.7M\",\n560\t            73.8,\n561\t            80.2,\n562\t            76.8,\n563\t            75.4,\n564\t            76.55,\n565\t            68.1\n566\t          ],\n567\t          [\n568\t            \"LoRA-V\",\n569\t            \"~2M\",\n570\t            75.2,\n571\t            82.0,\n572\t            78.4,\n573\t            77.6,\n574\t            78.3,\n575\t            67.4\n576\t          ],\n577\t          [\n578\t            \"Meta-Brain\",\n579\t            \"~6M\",\n580\t            71.0,\n581\t            77.6,\n582\t            74.4,\n583\t            73.0,\n584\t            74.0,\n585\t            71.4\n586\t          ],\n587\t          [\n588\t            \"Universal\",\n589\t            \"~2M\",\n590\t            62.8,\n591\t            69.4,\n592\t            66.2,\n593\t            63.0,\n594\t            65.35,\n595\t            65.4\n596\t          ]\n597\t        ]\n598\t      }\n599\t    ],\n600\t    \"figures\": [],\n601\t    \"analysis\": \"This experiment establishes two key points for the paper: (1) Visual-only methods are competitive on visual perturbations but fail as general robustness solutions — there is no free lunch. (2) Meta-Brain's routing approach provides the best overall coverage by handling all perturbation types, even though it doesn't maximize any single type. For Claim 3, FTM serves as an extreme point on the parameter-efficiency scatter plot: incredible visual-only efficiency but zero spatial/physical coverage. Meta-Brain occupies a qualitatively different position: moderate efficiency with universal coverage.\\n\\n\\nFTM at 4K params is remarkably parameter-efficient for visual perturbations (71.75% visual avg) but fundamentally cannot handle spatial/physical types, leaving those at frozen baseline levels and yielding only 65.3% overall. FLA at 4.7M params approaches LoRA-V quality on visual types (76.55% vs 78.3%) but also cannot generalize beyond visual. Meta-Brain's overall advantage (71.4% vs 65.3-68.1%) comes entirely from spatial and physical perturbation handling. On visual types alone, LoRA-V (78.3%) > FLA (76.55%) > Meta-Brain routed (74.0%) > FTM (71.75%), showing that specialized visual methods can match or exceed routing-based correction for visual perturbations specifically. The key message: FTM/FLA are excellent visual-only solutions but cannot serve as general robustness methods. Meta-Brain's value is breadth of coverage, not per-type optimality. FTM's per-parameter efficiency on visual types (11.7%/0.004M = 2925%/M) dwarfs all other methods but is limited to a single perturbation modality.\\n\"\n602\t  },\n603\t  {\n604\t    \"name\": \"Parameter Efficiency Analysis\",\n605\t    \"description\": \"Compute gain-over-frozen per method, normalize by trainable parameters. Produce the parameter efficiency scatter plot (x=params log scale, y=gain-over-baseline). Include Meta-Brain, Universal LoRA, FTM, FLA, VLA-GSE (from paper, different backbone caveat).\\n\",\n606\t    \"results\": {\n607\t      \"meta_brain_gain\": 17.8,\n608\t      \"meta_brain_params_M\": 6,\n609\t      \"meta_brain_gain_per_M\": 2.97,\n610\t      \"universal_gain\": 11.8,\n611\t      \"universal_params_M\": 2,\n612\t      \"universal_gain_per_M\": 5.9,\n613\t      \"ftm_gain\": 11.7,\n614\t      \"ftm_params_M\": 0.004,\n615\t      \"ftm_gain_per_M\": 2925.0,\n616\t      \"fla_gain\": 14.5,\n617\t      \"fla_params_M\": 4.7,\n618\t      \"fla_gain_per_M\": 3.09,\n619\t      \"vla_gse_gain\": 27.6,\n620\t      \"vla_gse_params_M\": 114,\n621\t      \"vla_gse_gain_per_M\": 0.24,\n622\t      \"meta_brain_pct_of_vla_gse_gain\": 64.5,\n623\t      \"meta_brain_pct_of_vla_gse_params\": 5.3\n624\t    },\n625\t    \"tables\": [\n626\t      {\n627\t        \"name\": \"Parameter Efficiency Comparison\",\n628\t        \"header\": [\n629\t          \"Method\",\n630\t          \"Backbone\",\n631\t          \"Params (M)\",\n632\t          \"Gain over Frozen (%)\",\n633\t          \"Gain/M Params\",\n634\t          \"Coverage\"\n635\t        ],\n636\t        \"rows\": [\n637\t          [\n638\t            \"FTM\",\n639\t            \"pi0\",\n640\t            0.004,\n641\t            11.7,\n642\t            2925.0,\n643\t            \"Visual only\"\n644\t          ],\n645\t          [\n646\t            \"Universal LoRA\",\n647\t            \"pi0\",\n648\t            2,\n649\t            11.8,\n650\t            5.9,\n651\t            \"All types\"\n652\t          ],\n653\t          [\n654\t            \"FLA\",\n655\t            \"pi0\",\n656\t            4.7,\n657\t            14.5,\n658\t            3.09,\n659\t            \"Visual only\"\n660\t          ],\n661\t          [\n662\t            \"Meta-Brain\",\n663\t            \"pi0\",\n664\t            6,\n665\t            17.8,\n666\t            2.97,\n667\t            \"All types\"\n668\t          ],\n669\t          [\n670\t            \"VLA-GSE†\",\n671\t            \"Qwen3-VL-4B\",\n672\t            114,\n673\t            27.6,\n674\t            0.24,\n675\t            \"All types\"\n676\t          ]\n677\t        ]\n678\t      }\n679\t    ],\n680\t    \"figures\": [],\n681\t    \"analysis\": \"The scatter plot is the visual centerpiece of Claim 3. The key insight is that Meta-Brain extends the Pareto frontier established by Universal LoRA — it proves that routing-based specialization extracts more gain per parameter than simply increasing uniform LoRA rank. The framing should be \\\"complementary to capacity routing, not replacement\\\": Meta-Brain operates in a different region of the parameter-efficiency space than VLA-GSE. For resource-constrained deployment (edge devices, real-time control), Meta-Brain offers a practical operating point. An ideal system might compose both: Meta-Brain for type-aware correction + capacity routing for residual error.\\n\\n\\nMeta-Brain achieves 2.97%/M gain-per-parameter, sitting in the middle of the efficiency spectrum. Universal LoRA is the most efficient multi-type method (5.9%/M) but has a lower absolute gain ceiling. FTM is an extreme outlier (2925%/M) demonstrating that ultra-compact methods exist for single-type perturbations, but this efficiency doesn't scale to multi-type coverage. VLA-GSE achieves the highest absolute gain (27.6%) but at 0.24%/M efficiency — a 12× parameter overhead vs Meta-Brain for 55% more gain. The headline for Claim 3: \\\"Meta-Brain achieves 64.5% of VLA-GSE's robustness improvement using 5.3% of its trainable parameters, demonstrating perturbation-diagnostic routing as a parameter-efficient alternative to capacity-based expert routing.\\\" The different-backbone caveat (pi0 vs Qwen3-VL-4B) applies to absolute numbers but the efficiency ratio is still informative.\\n\"\n682\t  },\n683\t  {\n684\t    \"name\": \"Correction Memory Ablation — With/Without CM\",\n685\t    \"description\": \"Compare Meta-Brain with and without Correction Memory module. CM modulates routing weights using retrieved exemplars from a FAISS-indexed memory bank. Quantify marginal gain overall and per perturbation type.\\n\",\n686\t    \"results\": {\n687\t      \"meta_brain_no_cm\": 71.4,\n688\t      \"meta_brain_with_cm\": 72.6,\n689\t      \"cm_ablation_delta\": 1.2,\n690\t      \"cm_delta_visual\": 0.8,\n691\t      \"cm_delta_spatial\": 0.4,\n692\t      \"cm_delta_physical\": 2.8,\n693\t      \"cm_alpha_learned\": 0.23,\n694\t      \"cm_retrieval_latency_ms\": 1.4\n695\t    },\n696\t    \"tables\": [\n697\t      {\n698\t        \"name\": \"Correction Memory Ablation (%)\",\n699\t        \"header\": [\n700\t          \"Configuration\",\n701\t          \"Visual\",\n702\t          \"Spatial\",\n703\t          \"Physical\",\n704\t          \"Overall\"\n705\t        ],\n706\t        \"rows\": [\n707\t          [\n708\t            \"Meta-Brain (no CM)\",\n709\t            74.0,\n710\t            64.8,\n711\t            74.2,\n712\t            71.4\n713\t          ],\n714\t          [\n715\t            \"Meta-Brain + CM\",\n716\t            74.8,\n717\t            65.2,\n718\t            77.0,\n719\t            72.6\n720\t          ],\n721\t          [\n722\t            \"CM Delta\",\n723\t            0.8,\n724\t            0.4,\n725\t            2.8,\n726\t            1.2\n727\t          ]\n728\t        ]\n729\t      }\n730\t    ],\n731\t    \"figures\": [],\n732\t    \"analysis\": \"CM's marginal contribution (1.2%) is expected in the median tier. It validates the module as a sound idea (positive delta on all types) but not a paper-defining contribution. Present CM as \\\"optional module, +1.2% overall, primarily benefiting recurrent physical perturbations.\\\" The per-type pattern adds a small mechanistic insight: exemplar-based routing modulation works best when perturbations are consistent (physics changes) rather than combinatorially varied (spatial layouts). For the paper, CM goes in the ablation section with 1-2 sentences, not a dedicated section.\\n\\n\\nCM provides a marginal +1.2% overall gain, concentrated on physical perturbations (+2.8%) where the same robot dynamics changes recur consistently across episodes — exemplar retrieval identifies \\\"I've seen this perturbation before\\\" and sharpens the routing weight toward LoRA-P. Visual gain is small (+0.8%) because the PAR router already routes visual perturbations confidently (diag 0.82) and CM's modulation has little room to improve. Spatial gain is minimal (+0.4%) because spatial perturbations are fundamentally different each time (different object positions) so retrieved exemplars provide weak signal. The learned alpha parameter (0.23) confirms the system's own assessment: CM contributes ~23% weighting to the final routing decision, indicating it's a secondary correction to PAR, not a primary signal. The 1.4ms retrieval latency adds negligible overhead to the 10Hz control loop. CM is worth including as an optional module but does not change the core story.\\n\"\n733\t  },\n734\t  {\n735\t    \"name\": \"Router Architecture Comparison — MLP vs Linear vs Attention\",\n736\t    \"description\": \"Compare PAR router variants on PaliGemma layer 6: (a) 2-layer MLP (default, ~50K params), (b) linear classifier (~6K params), (c) cross-attention router (~120K params). Evaluate both routing accuracy and downstream task success to test whether classification accuracy improvements translate to task performance.\\n\",\n737\t    \"results\": {\n738\t      \"mlp_routing_accuracy\": 93.8,\n739\t      \"mlp_task_success\": 71.4,\n740\t      \"mlp_params\": 50000,\n741\t      \"linear_routing_accuracy\": 90.6,\n742\t      \"linear_task_success\": 70.2,\n743\t      \"linear_params\": 6000,\n744\t      \"attention_routing_accuracy\": 94.2,\n745\t      \"attention_task_success\": 71.2,\n746\t      \"attention_params\": 120000,\n747\t      \"mlp_vs_linear_task_gap\": 1.2,\n748\t      \"mlp_vs_attention_task_gap\": 0.2\n749\t    },\n750\t    \"tables\": [\n751\t      {\n752\t        \"name\": \"Router Architecture Comparison\",\n753\t        \"header\": [\n754\t          \"Router\",\n755\t          \"Params\",\n756\t          \"Routing Acc (%)\",\n757\t          \"Task Success (%)\",\n758\t          \"Inference Overhead (ms)\"\n759\t        ],\n760\t        \"rows\": [\n761\t          [\n762\t            \"Linear\",\n763\t            \"6K\",\n764\t            90.6,\n765\t            70.2,\n766\t            0.02\n767\t          ],\n768\t          [\n769\t            \"MLP (default)\",\n770\t            \"50K\",\n771\t            93.8,\n772\t            71.4,\n773\t            0.05\n774\t          ],\n775\t          [\n776\t            \"Cross-Attention\",\n777\t            \"120K\",\n778\t            94.2,\n779\t            71.2,\n780\t            0.18\n781\t          ]\n782\t        ]\n783\t      }\n784\t    ],\n785\t    \"figures\": [],\n786\t    \"analysis\": \"This experiment addresses reviewer concern C1 (\\\"Phase 0 classification is too easy\\\") from a different angle: yes, the classification is relatively easy (even a linear classifier gets 90.6%), but the task-success gap between 90.6% and 93.8% routing accuracy is 1.2% — confirming that routing accuracy does matter at the margins. The MLP's 3.2% classification advantage over linear translates to 1.2% task success gain, roughly 1:0.37 ratio. Beyond 93.8%, additional classification gains have near-zero marginal task benefit. This helps calibrate the oracle-PAR gap analysis: the 3.9% task gap from oracle routing quality corresponds to moving from ~93.8% to ~100% classification accuracy, and the nonlinear mapping suggests diminishing returns as routing approaches perfection.\\n\\n\\nMLP (default) achieves 93.8% routing accuracy and 71.4% task success, offering the best cost/performance tradeoff. Linear router loses 3.2% routing accuracy, which translates to a modest 1.2% task success drop — the per-token representation at layer 6 is already quite separable (Phase 0 showed F1=0.993 with MLP), so a linear boundary captures most of the structure but misses some nonlinear decision boundaries near cluster edges. Attention router adds 0.4% routing accuracy over MLP (the classes are well-separated enough that attention over token sequences adds minimal information beyond mean pooling) but this doesn't translate to task success improvement (71.2% vs 71.4%) — the 0.4% classification gain falls within the noise floor of rollout evaluation. The attention router's 2.4× parameter overhead is unjustified. Conclusion: MLP is the right choice, confirming the Phase 0 finding that perturbation type is a simple classification problem at layer 6.\\n\"\n787\t  },\n788\t  {\n789\t    \"name\": \"6-Class vs 3-Class Routing Comparison\",\n790\t    \"description\": \"Compare fine-grained 6-class routing (camera/light/background/noise/layout/robot, 6 LoRA experts ~12M params) with coarse 3-class routing (Visual/Spatial/Physical, 3 experts ~6M). Tests whether finer-grained perturbation-type routing justifies additional parameters and architectural complexity.\\n\",\n791\t    \"results\": {\n792\t      \"three_class_overall\": 71.4,\n793\t      \"three_class_routing_accuracy\": 93.8,\n794\t      \"three_class_params_M\": 6,\n795\t      \"six_class_overall\": 72.2,\n796\t      \"six_class_routing_accuracy\": 89.4,\n797\t      \"six_class_params_M\": 12,\n798\t      \"delta_overall\": 0.8,\n799\t      \"six_class_camera\": 72.8,\n800\t      \"six_class_light\": 79.2,\n801\t      \"six_class_background\": 76.0,\n802\t      \"six_class_noise\": 74.6,\n803\t      \"six_class_distractor\": 66.0,\n804\t      \"six_class_layout\": 65.2,\n805\t      \"six_class_robot\": 81.6\n806\t    },\n807\t    \"tables\": [\n808\t      {\n809\t        \"name\": \"6-Class vs 3-Class Comparison (%)\",\n810\t        \"header\": [\n811\t          \"Configuration\",\n812\t          \"Classes\",\n813\t          \"Params\",\n814\t          \"Routing Acc\",\n815\t          \"Overall\",\n816\t          \"Δ vs 3-class\"\n817\t        ],\n818\t        \"rows\": [\n819\t          [\n820\t            \"3-class Meta-Brain\",\n821\t            3,\n822\t            \"~6M\",\n823\t            93.8,\n824\t            71.4,\n825\t            \"—\"\n826\t          ],\n827\t          [\n828\t            \"6-class Meta-Brain\",\n829\t            6,\n830\t            \"~12M\",\n831\t            89.4,\n832\t            72.2,\n833\t            0.8\n834\t          ]\n835\t        ]\n836\t      },\n837\t      {\n838\t        \"name\": \"6-Class Per-Type Breakdown vs 3-Class (%)\",\n839\t        \"header\": [\n840\t          \"Perturbation\",\n841\t          \"3-class\",\n842\t          \"6-class\",\n843\t          \"Delta\"\n844\t        ],\n845\t        \"rows\": [\n846\t          [\n847\t            \"camera\",\n848\t            71.0,\n849\t            72.8,\n850\t            1.8\n851\t          ],\n852\t          [\n853\t            \"light\",\n854\t            77.6,\n855\t            79.2,\n856\t            1.6\n857\t          ],\n858\t          [\n859\t            \"background\",\n860\t            74.4,\n861\t            76.0,\n862\t            1.6\n863\t          ],\n864\t          [\n865\t            \"noise\",\n866\t            73.0,\n867\t            74.6,\n868\t            1.6\n869\t          ],\n870\t          [\n871\t            \"distractor\",\n872\t            65.2,\n873\t            66.0,\n874\t            0.8\n875\t          ],\n876\t          [\n877\t            \"layout\",\n878\t            64.4,\n879\t            65.2,\n880\t            0.8\n881\t          ],\n882\t          [\n883\t            \"robot\",\n884\t            74.2,\n885\t            81.6,\n886\t            7.4\n887\t          ]\n888\t        ]\n889\t      }\n890\t    ],\n891\t    \"figures\": [],\n892\t    \"analysis\": \"This directly addresses reviewer concern C5 (\\\"3-class taxonomy may be too coarse\\\"). The answer is: 3-class is nearly optimal for this architecture. The 6-class robot improvement (+7.4%) is notable in isolation but doesn't offset the systemic costs of finer routing. The Phase 0 intra-Visual similarity finding (6-class accuracy 99.82% but intra-Visual confusion is near-zero) predicted this outcome: types that cluster together in activation space don't need separate experts. Include this as a 1-paragraph ablation result: \\\"Finer 6-class routing adds +0.8% at 2× parameters, confirming that the 3-class taxonomy aligns with the mechanistic distinction in VLA processing stages.\\\"\\n\\n\\n6-class routing provides only +0.8% overall improvement over 3-class while doubling the parameter budget (12M vs 6M) and dropping routing accuracy by 4.4% (89.4% vs 93.8%). The marginal gain arises primarily from the robot expert (+3.8% from dedicated robot LoRA vs shared Physical LoRA) where the 1-type Physical class benefits from fully dedicated parameters. Intra-Visual perturbation types (camera/light/background/noise) do NOT benefit significantly from separate experts because they share the same failure mode (early representation corruption) — Phase 0 6-class analysis already showed high intra-Visual similarity. The spatial split (distractor/layout) gains marginally because both types require trajectory-level reasoning that LoRA cannot provide. The 4.4% routing accuracy drop with 6 classes partially offsets any per-expert specialization gain: more classes means more routing errors, and each error routes to a more narrowly specialized (thus more harmful when mismatched) expert. Net assessment: 3-class is the better design point.\\n\"\n893\t  },\n894\t  {\n895\t    \"name\": \"Cross-Backbone Validation — SmolVLA\",\n896\t    \"description\": \"Replicate Phase 0 classification + Phase 1 routing pipeline on SmolVLA to test whether perturbation-diagnostic routing generalizes beyond pi0. SmolVLA uses a different vision encoder (SigLIP) and language backbone (SmolLM), testing architecture independence.\\n\",\n897\t    \"results\": {\n898\t      \"smolvla_phase0_3class_f1\": 0.938,\n899\t      \"smolvla_phase0_best_layer\": 4,\n900\t      \"smolvla_frozen_baseline\": 46.2,\n901\t      \"smolvla_universal_lora\": 56.8,\n902\t      \"smolvla_meta_brain\": 62.4,\n903\t      \"smolvla_gain_over_frozen\": 16.2,\n904\t      \"smolvla_gain_over_universal\": 5.6,\n905\t      \"smolvla_routing_diagonality\": 0.73,\n906\t      \"smolvla_visual_specialization_delta\": 10.4,\n907\t      \"smolvla_spatial_specialization_delta\": 2.8,\n908\t      \"smolvla_physical_specialization_delta\": 7.6\n909\t    },\n910\t    \"tables\": [\n911\t      {\n912\t        \"name\": \"Cross-Backbone Comparison (pi0 vs SmolVLA)\",\n913\t        \"header\": [\n914\t          \"Metric\",\n915\t          \"pi0\",\n916\t          \"SmolVLA\"\n917\t        ],\n918\t        \"rows\": [\n919\t          [\n920\t            \"Phase 0 3-class F1\",\n921\t            0.993,\n922\t            0.938\n923\t          ],\n924\t          [\n925\t            \"Best classification layer\",\n926\t            6,\n927\t            4\n928\t          ],\n929\t          [\n930\t            \"Frozen baseline (%)\",\n931\t            53.6,\n932\t            46.2\n933\t          ],\n934\t          [\n935\t            \"Universal LoRA (%)\",\n936\t            65.4,\n937\t            56.8\n938\t          ],\n939\t          [\n940\t            \"Meta-Brain (%)\",\n941\t            71.4,\n942\t            62.4\n943\t          ],\n944\t          [\n945\t            \"Gain over frozen (%)\",\n946\t            17.8,\n947\t            16.2\n948\t          ],\n949\t          [\n950\t            \"Gain over universal (%)\",\n951\t            6.0,\n952\t            5.6\n953\t          ],\n954\t          [\n955\t            \"Routing diagonality\",\n956\t            0.77,\n957\t            0.73\n958\t          ],\n959\t          [\n960\t            \"Visual specialization delta (%)\",\n961\t            12.95,\n962\t            10.4\n963\t          ],\n964\t          [\n965\t            \"Spatial specialization delta (%)\",\n966\t            4.3,\n967\t            2.8\n968\t          ]\n969\t        ]\n970\t      }\n971\t    ],\n972\t    \"figures\": [],\n973\t    \"analysis\": \"This experiment addresses reviewer concern C3 (\\\"single backbone, limited generalizability\\\"). The result is clearly positive: the qualitative pattern replicates exactly (same per-type specialization ranking, same ablation ordering, comparable relative gains). The Phase 0 F1 drop (0.993 → 0.938) is expected — SigLIP and PaliGemma have different feature structures — but 0.938 is still high enough for effective routing. The spatial underperformance replication across backbones strengthens the mechanistic interpretation: it's a property of the perturbation type, not the backbone. For the paper, include as a validation subsection: \\\"Cross-backbone results on SmolVLA replicate the qualitative findings, with comparable routing gains (+5.6% vs +6.0% over universal), confirming that perturbation-diagnostic routing is an architecture-independent principle.\\\" This is a P2 experiment — include if time/GPU budget permits, otherwise cite the Phase 0 cross-backbone evidence from SAFE/probing literature.\\n\\n\\nSmolVLA confirms the core finding: frozen VLA activations encode perturbation type (F1=0.938, lower than pi0's 0.993 due to SigLIP's different feature structure but still high). The best classification layer shifts to layer 4 (SmolVLA has fewer layers than PaliGemma). Meta-Brain on SmolVLA achieves +16.2% gain over frozen (comparable to pi0's +17.8%) and +5.6% over universal (comparable to pi0's +6.0%), demonstrating that the routing benefit is architecture-independent. Absolute numbers are lower (62.4% vs 71.4%) due to SmolVLA's weaker backbone capacity (SmolLM-360M vs pi0's larger model). The per-type specialization pattern replicates: visual shows the largest delta (10.4%), physical moderate (7.6%), spatial weak (2.8%). Spatial underperformance persists across backbones, confirming it's a fundamental limitation of single-step LoRA correction for trajectory-level perturbations, not an artifact of pi0's architecture. Routing diagonality (0.73) is slightly lower than pi0 (0.77), reflecting SigLIP's less structured intermediate representations for perturbation classification.\\n\"\n974\t  },\n975\t  {\n976\t    \"name\": \"LoRA Layer Placement Ablation\",\n977\t    \"description\": \"Vary which PaliGemma layers receive LoRA: (a) early (2-6), (b) mid (6-12), (c) late (12-16), (d) all layers. Test hypothesis that perturbation types optimally correct at different processing stages.\\n\",\n978\t    \"results\": {\n979\t      \"lora_v_early\": 80.2,\n980\t      \"lora_v_mid\": 74.8,\n981\t      \"lora_v_late\": 71.4,\n982\t      \"lora_v_all\": 78.3,\n983\t      \"lora_s_early\": 64.6,\n984\t      \"lora_s_mid\": 70.2,\n985\t      \"lora_s_late\": 66.8,\n986\t      \"lora_s_all\": 68.0,\n987\t      \"lora_p_early\": 72.8,\n988\t      \"lora_p_mid\": 75.2,\n989\t      \"lora_p_late\": 80.6,\n990\t      \"lora_p_all\": 78.0,\n991\t      \"optimal_placement_v\": \"early (2-6)\",\n992\t      \"optimal_placement_s\": \"mid (6-12)\",\n993\t      \"optimal_placement_p\": \"late (12-16)\",\n994\t      \"optimal_combined_overall\": 73.8,\n995\t      \"all_layer_combined_overall\": 71.4,\n996\t      \"layer_placement_gain\": 2.4\n997\t    },\n998\t    \"tables\": [\n999\t      {\n1000\t        \"name\": \"Layer Placement × Expert Type (own-type success rate %)\",\n1001\t        \"header\": [\n1002\t          \"Layer Range\",\n1003\t          \"LoRA-V (Visual)\",\n1004\t          \"LoRA-S (Spatial)\",\n1005\t          \"LoRA-P (Physical)\"\n1006\t        ],\n1007\t        \"rows\": [\n1008\t          [\n1009\t            \"Early (2-6)\",\n1010\t            80.2,\n1011\t            64.6,\n1012\t            72.8\n1013\t          ],\n1014\t          [\n1015\t            \"Mid (6-12)\",\n1016\t            74.8,\n1017\t            70.2,\n1018\t            75.2\n1019\t          ],\n1020\t          [\n1021\t            \"Late (12-16)\",\n1022\t            71.4,\n1023\t            66.8,\n1024\t            80.6\n1025\t          ],\n1026\t          [\n1027\t            \"All layers\",\n1028\t            78.3,\n1029\t            68.0,\n1030\t            78.0\n1031\t          ]\n1032\t        ]\n1033\t      },\n1034\t      {\n1035\t        \"name\": \"Optimal Layer Placement Summary\",\n1036\t        \"header\": [\n1037\t          \"Expert\",\n1038\t          \"Best Layers\",\n1039\t          \"Own-Type Success\",\n1040\t          \"Δ vs All-Layer\"\n1041\t        ],\n1042\t        \"rows\": [\n1043\t          [\n1044\t            \"LoRA-V\",\n1045\t            \"Early (2-6)\",\n1046\t            80.2,\n1047\t            1.9\n1048\t          ],\n1049\t          [\n1050\t            \"LoRA-S\",\n1051\t            \"Mid (6-12)\",\n1052\t            70.2,\n1053\t            2.2\n1054\t          ],\n1055\t          [\n1056\t            \"LoRA-P\",\n1057\t            \"Late (12-16)\",\n1058\t            80.6,\n1059\t            2.6\n1060\t          ]\n1061\t        ]\n1062\t      }\n1063\t    ],\n1064\t    \"figures\": [],\n1065\t    \"analysis\": \"This is a P2 experiment that adds mechanistic depth but is not essential for the core claims. The crossed peak pattern (V-early, S-mid, P-late) aligns with how VLA processing stages work: visual encoding → spatial reasoning → action prediction. This provides evidence for the paper's framing that perturbation types disrupt different processing stages. The +2.4% gain from optimal layer placement is small but suggests a natural extension: layer-aware routing that not only selects the right expert but applies it at the right layers. This could close part of the oracle-PAR gap (3.9%) without improving classification accuracy. For the paper, include as a supplementary analysis with the crossed-peak figure — it's visually compelling and mechanistically interpretive. Do not pursue layer-specific routing as a primary contribution; it's a natural future work direction.\\n\\nThe layer placement results support the mechanistic hypothesis with moderate effect sizes. LoRA-V benefits most from early layers (+1.9% over all-layer) because visual perturbations (camera/light/background/noise) corrupt the initial visual encoding in PaliGemma's early layers — correcting at the source prevents error propagation. LoRA-S benefits from mid layers (+2.2% over all-layer) because spatial reasoning (object relationships, layout understanding) is computed in middle transformer layers where multi-head attention integrates spatial context — though the absolute improvement remains modest due to the fundamental trajectory-level limitation. LoRA-P benefits from late layers (+2.6% over all-layer) because physical perturbations manifest at the action prediction stage in late layers where dynamics models are implicitly computed. All-layer placement performs as a reasonable default (second-best for each expert type) because the LoRA rank is distributed across layers that contribute varying amounts. The combined optimal placement (type-specific layers per expert) yields 73.8% overall (+2.4% over uniform all-layer 71.4%), suggesting layer-aware routing is a promising extension.\\n\"\n1066\t  }\n1067\t]"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f10e81d9-0851-41df-87e8-bf73f3c49003","uuid":"2cdebc87-88bd-439a-993d-03b776049b31","timestamp":"2026-05-15T16:18:45.034Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-be-idea-gen-0515/projects/meta_brain_lora_routing/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260515T154653/median/experiment_data.json","content":"[\n  {\n    \"name\": \"LoRA Expert Training — Specialized vs Universal\",\n    \"description\": \"Train 4 rank-16 LoRA adapters on frozen pi0: LoRA-V (visual data), LoRA-S (spatial data), LoRA-P (physical data), Universal (mixed all-type data). Evaluate each on all 7 perturbation types to obtain the cross-type performance matrix.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"lora_v_visual_avg\": 78.3,\n      \"lora_v_camera\": 75.2,\n      \"lora_v_light\": 82.0,\n      \"lora_v_background\": 78.4,\n      \"lora_v_noise\": 77.6,\n      \"lora_v_spatial_avg\": 54.0,\n      \"lora_v_physical\": 50.6,\n      \"lora_s_spatial_avg\": 68.0,\n      \"lora_s_distractor\": 69.4,\n      \"lora_s_layout\": 66.6,\n      \"lora_s_visual_avg\": 46.9,\n      \"lora_s_physical\": 49.2,\n      \"lora_p_physical\": 78.0,\n      \"lora_p_visual_avg\": 47.9,\n      \"lora_p_spatial_avg\": 53.7,\n      \"universal_overall\": 65.4,\n      \"universal_visual_avg\": 65.35,\n      \"universal_spatial_avg\": 63.7,\n      \"universal_physical\": 69.0,\n      \"specialization_delta_visual\": 12.95,\n      \"specialization_delta_spatial\": 4.3,\n      \"specialization_delta_physical\": 9.0,\n      \"specialization_delta_max\": 12.95\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Cross-Type Expert Performance Matrix (success rate %)\",\n        \"header\": [\n          \"Method\",\n          \"camera\",\n          \"light\",\n          \"background\",\n          \"noise\",\n          \"distractor\",\n          \"layout\",\n          \"robot\",\n          \"Overall\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Frozen pi0\",\n            47.6,\n            56.2,\n            51.8,\n            49.4,\n            58.6,\n            57.8,\n            53.8,\n            53.6\n          ],\n          [\n            \"LoRA-V\",\n            75.2,\n            82.0,\n            78.4,\n            77.6,\n            54.2,\n            53.8,\n            50.6,\n            67.4\n          ],\n          [\n            \"LoRA-S\",\n            44.2,\n            50.4,\n            47.8,\n            45.0,\n            69.4,\n            66.6,\n            49.2,\n            53.2\n          ],\n          [\n            \"LoRA-P\",\n            45.6,\n            51.2,\n            48.4,\n            46.2,\n            54.0,\n            53.4,\n            78.0,\n            53.8\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Universal\",\n            62.8,\n            69.4,\n            66.2,\n            63.0,\n            64.8,\n            62.6,\n            69.0,\n            65.4\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Specialization Delta (expert own-type minus universal same-type)\",\n        \"header\": [\n          \"Coarse Type\",\n          \"Expert Own-Type\",\n          \"Universal Same-Type\",\n          \"Delta\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Visual\",\n            78.3,\n            65.35,\n            12.95\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Spatial\",\n            68.0,\n            63.7,\n            4.3\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Physical\",\n            78.0,\n            69.0,\n            9.0\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The cross-type matrix is the foundational evidence for why routing matters: correct expert helps significantly (+9 to +13% over universal), wrong expert hurts severely (below frozen). This asymmetry means random or poor routing can be worse than no routing at all, motivating the need for accurate PAR classification. The weak spatial specialization is interpretable: layout and distractor perturbations change the goal-relevant spatial configuration, requiring trajectory-level replanning that a single-step representation correction cannot provide. This is a fundamental limitation of the LoRA-based approach for spatial perturbations, not a routing failure. The universal LoRA's surprising strength (65.4%) sets a high bar for Meta-Brain — the routing system must do substantially better to justify 3× the parameters.\\n\\n\\nVisual expert (LoRA-V) shows the strongest specialization (+13% over universal on visual types) because visual perturbations (camera/light/background/noise) corrupt early PaliGemma representations in a consistent, localized manner — rank-16 LoRA can learn a compact correction to these early-layer distortions. Physical expert (LoRA-P) shows strong but smaller specialization (+9%) because robot perturbations (changed dynamics/mass) affect action-space outputs which are more distributed across layers. Spatial expert (LoRA-S) shows weak specialization (+4.3%) because layout and distractor perturbations fundamentally change the required trajectory — not just a representational shift but a different action sequence — and single-step LoRA correction at the representation level cannot replan a trajectory. Cross-type performance degrades sharply (below frozen baseline) because each specialized expert learns type-specific correction biases that actively harm representations when applied to the wrong perturbation type. Universal LoRA at 65.4% is stronger than expected because mixed training provides partial cross-type robustness — the LoRA learns an average correction that modestly helps all types without hurting any.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Meta-Brain Assembly — PAR Router + LoRA Bank\",\n    \"description\": \"Assemble PAR router (PaliGemma layer 6, 2-layer MLP, initialized from Phase 0 weights) with 3 LoRA experts. Soft routing: h_corrected = h_frozen + Σ_k p(type_k) · LoRA_k(h_frozen). End-to-end routing fine-tuning with frozen LoRA parameters (only tune PAR weights). Evaluate on full LIBERO-Plus benchmark.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"libero_plus_overall\": 71.4,\n      \"per_type_camera\": 71.0,\n      \"per_type_light\": 77.6,\n      \"per_type_background\": 74.4,\n      \"per_type_noise\": 73.0,\n      \"per_type_distractor\": 65.2,\n      \"per_type_layout\": 64.4,\n      \"per_type_robot\": 74.2,\n      \"visual_avg\": 74.0,\n      \"spatial_avg\": 64.8,\n      \"physical\": 74.2,\n      \"gain_over_frozen\": 17.8,\n      \"gain_over_universal\": 6.0,\n      \"routing_diag_visual\": 0.82,\n      \"routing_diag_spatial\": 0.68,\n      \"routing_diag_physical\": 0.8,\n      \"routing_diagonality_mean\": 0.77,\n      \"hard_assignment_accuracy\": 89.0\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Meta-Brain Per-Perturbation Success Rate (%)\",\n        \"header\": [\n          \"Perturbation\",\n          \"Frozen\",\n          \"Universal\",\n          \"Meta-Brain\",\n          \"Gain over Universal\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"camera\",\n            47.6,\n            62.8,\n            71.0,\n            8.2\n          ],\n          [\n            \"light\",\n            56.2,\n            69.4,\n            77.6,\n            8.2\n          ],\n          [\n            \"background\",\n            51.8,\n            66.2,\n            74.4,\n            8.2\n          ],\n          [\n            \"noise\",\n            49.4,\n            63.0,\n            73.0,\n            10.0\n          ],\n          [\n            \"distractor\",\n            58.6,\n            64.8,\n            65.2,\n            0.4\n          ],\n          [\n            \"layout\",\n            57.8,\n            62.6,\n            64.4,\n            1.8\n          ],\n          [\n            \"robot\",\n            53.8,\n            69.0,\n            74.2,\n            5.2\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Overall\",\n            53.6,\n            65.4,\n            71.4,\n            6.0\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"PAR Soft Routing Weight Matrix (mean weights per true type)\",\n        \"header\": [\n          \"True Type\",\n          \"P(LoRA-V)\",\n          \"P(LoRA-S)\",\n          \"P(LoRA-P)\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Visual\",\n            0.82,\n            0.06,\n            0.12\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Spatial\",\n            0.2,\n            0.68,\n            0.12\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Physical\",\n            0.12,\n            0.08,\n            0.8\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Meta-Brain achieves the target ordering (Meta-Brain > Universal > Frozen on all 7 types) but the margin varies dramatically: +8-10% on visual types, +5% on physical, only +0.4-1.8% on spatial. This asymmetry is mechanistically interpretable: visual perturbations corrupt the representation in a way that a type-specific LoRA can precisely invert, while spatial perturbations require policy-level replanning beyond representation correction. The routing weight analysis reveals the PAR router appropriately lacks confidence on spatial inputs (diag 0.68), which limits both the benefit and the harm of routing on these types. The end-to-end routing fine-tuning shifts the router from pure classification toward task-success optimization, explaining the gap between Phase 0 accuracy (99.3%) and deployed routing accuracy (~89%).\\n\\n\\nSoft routing dilutes the correction signal compared to hard (oracle) routing, costing ~4% overall. The dilution is uniform across types (3-5% per type) because even a small weight on the wrong expert introduces harmful correction biases. Spatial routing diagonality is lowest (0.68) because layout/distractor perturbations share visual features with camera/background changes at layer 6 — the PAR router's operating point. Despite this, Meta-Brain still improves over universal on spatial (+1.1%) because even imperfect soft routing toward LoRA-S is better than no type awareness. Visual gain (+8.65% over universal) is large because high routing confidence (0.82) allows LoRA-V to dominate the correction. Physical gain (+5.2%) is moderate due to good routing (0.80) but more distributed perturbation effects. Hard assignment accuracy (89%) is lower than Phase 0 classification (99.3%) because the router is fine-tuned for task success, not classification accuracy — it learns to soften assignments when mixing provides marginal task benefit.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Oracle Routing — Upper Bound for Routing Quality\",\n    \"description\": \"Replace PAR soft router with ground-truth perturbation type labels (hard 1-hot routing to the correct expert). Same LoRA experts from plan_002. Measures the ceiling for routing quality and quantifies PAR's gap to perfect routing.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"oracle_overall\": 75.3,\n      \"oracle_camera\": 75.2,\n      \"oracle_light\": 82.0,\n      \"oracle_background\": 78.4,\n      \"oracle_noise\": 77.6,\n      \"oracle_distractor\": 69.4,\n      \"oracle_layout\": 66.6,\n      \"oracle_robot\": 78.0,\n      \"oracle_par_gap_overall\": 3.9,\n      \"oracle_par_gap_visual\": 4.3,\n      \"oracle_par_gap_spatial\": 3.8,\n      \"oracle_par_gap_physical\": 3.8\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Oracle vs PAR Per-Type Gap (%)\",\n        \"header\": [\n          \"Perturbation\",\n          \"Oracle\",\n          \"Meta-Brain (PAR)\",\n          \"Gap\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"camera\",\n            75.2,\n            71.0,\n            4.2\n          ],\n          [\n            \"light\",\n            82.0,\n            77.6,\n            4.4\n          ],\n          [\n            \"background\",\n            78.4,\n            74.4,\n            4.0\n          ],\n          [\n            \"noise\",\n            77.6,\n            73.0,\n            4.6\n          ],\n          [\n            \"distractor\",\n            69.4,\n            65.2,\n            4.2\n          ],\n          [\n            \"layout\",\n            66.6,\n            64.4,\n            2.2\n          ],\n          [\n            \"robot\",\n            78.0,\n            74.2,\n            3.8\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Overall\",\n            75.3,\n            71.4,\n            3.9\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The oracle-PAR gap of 3.9% is in the moderate range — large enough to matter for absolute performance but not large enough to invalidate the routing approach. The implication for Claim 2 is important: even with perfect routing, the gain over universal LoRA would be 75.3 - 65.4 = 9.9%, only ~64% more than Meta-Brain's 6.0%. This means routing quality accounts for ~40% of the potential routing benefit left on the table. For the paper, frame this as: \\\"PAR captures ~60% of the theoretically available routing benefit, with the remaining gap attributable to soft-routing dilution and spatial ambiguity.\\\" The gap also establishes headroom for future router improvements (e.g., confidence-gated hard routing).\\n\\n\\nOracle routing eliminates soft-routing dilution entirely — each perturbation type receives 100% correction from its matched expert, with zero contamination from mismatched experts. The 3.9% overall gap from Meta-Brain to oracle quantifies the cost of learned soft routing versus perfect hard routing. This gap arises from two sources: (1) non-zero weight on wrong experts injects harmful correction biases (~60% of the gap), and (2) the router's lower confidence on spatial perturbations causes LoRA-S to be under-weighted even on true spatial inputs (~40% of the gap). The gap is moderate (not catastrophic), indicating PAR routing is functional but not near-perfect. Closing this gap further would require either better features for spatial classification at layer 6, or a hybrid routing scheme that uses hard routing when confidence is high and universal fallback when confidence is low. Note that oracle performance (75.3%) still trails VLA-GSE (81.2%) by 5.9%, confirming that the performance ceiling is set by pi0 backbone capacity and LoRA rank, not routing quality.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Ablation Ladder — Frozen → Universal → Meta-Brain → Oracle\",\n    \"description\": \"Systematic 5-seed comparison on full LIBERO-Plus: Frozen pi0, Universal LoRA (rank 16, ~2M), Meta-Brain (PAR + 3 LoRA, ~6M), Oracle routing (ground-truth type). Report overall and per-perturbation breakdown with statistical significance. Core evidence for Claim 2.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"frozen_mean\": 53.6,\n      \"frozen_std\": 1.8,\n      \"universal_mean\": 65.4,\n      \"universal_std\": 2.1,\n      \"meta_brain_mean\": 71.4,\n      \"meta_brain_std\": 2.4,\n      \"oracle_mean\": 75.3,\n      \"oracle_std\": 1.9,\n      \"gain_frozen_to_universal\": 11.8,\n      \"gain_universal_to_meta_brain\": 6.0,\n      \"gain_meta_brain_to_oracle\": 3.9,\n      \"pvalue_universal_gt_frozen\": 0.0003,\n      \"pvalue_meta_brain_gt_universal\": 0.008,\n      \"pvalue_oracle_gt_meta_brain\": 0.021,\n      \"meta_brain_gain_over_frozen\": 17.8\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Ablation Ladder — Overall Success Rate (%, 5 seeds)\",\n        \"header\": [\n          \"Method\",\n          \"Params\",\n          \"Mean\",\n          \"Std\",\n          \"95% CI\",\n          \"p vs. Previous\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Frozen pi0\",\n            0,\n            53.6,\n            1.8,\n            \"[51.4, 55.8]\",\n            \"—\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Universal LoRA\",\n            \"~2M\",\n            65.4,\n            2.1,\n            \"[62.8, 68.0]\",\n            \"<0.001\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Meta-Brain\",\n            \"~6M\",\n            71.4,\n            2.4,\n            \"[68.4, 74.4]\",\n            \"0.008\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Oracle\",\n            \"~6M\",\n            75.3,\n            1.9,\n            \"[72.9, 77.7]\",\n            \"0.021\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Ablation Ladder — Per-Perturbation Breakdown (%, 5-seed mean)\",\n        \"header\": [\n          \"Method\",\n          \"camera\",\n          \"light\",\n          \"bg\",\n          \"noise\",\n          \"distr\",\n          \"layout\",\n          \"robot\",\n          \"Overall\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Frozen\",\n            47.6,\n            56.2,\n            51.8,\n            49.4,\n            58.6,\n            57.8,\n            53.8,\n            53.6\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Universal\",\n            62.8,\n            69.4,\n            66.2,\n            63.0,\n            64.8,\n            62.6,\n            69.0,\n            65.4\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Meta-Brain\",\n            71.0,\n            77.6,\n            74.4,\n            73.0,\n            65.2,\n            64.4,\n            74.2,\n            71.4\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Oracle\",\n            75.2,\n            82.0,\n            78.4,\n            77.6,\n            69.4,\n            66.6,\n            78.0,\n            75.3\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Routing Gain Decomposition by Coarse Type\",\n        \"header\": [\n          \"Coarse Type\",\n          \"Universal\",\n          \"Meta-Brain\",\n          \"Routing Gain\",\n          \"% of Oracle Ceiling\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Visual (4 types)\",\n            65.35,\n            74.0,\n            8.65,\n            \"66.8%\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Spatial (2 types)\",\n            63.7,\n            64.8,\n            1.1,\n            \"25.6%\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Physical (1 type)\",\n            69.0,\n            74.2,\n            5.2,\n            \"57.8%\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Overall\",\n            65.4,\n            71.4,\n            6.0,\n            \"60.6%\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The ablation ladder is clean and publishable. All pairwise comparisons are significant. The key narrative point is that Meta-Brain captures 60.6% of the theoretically available routing benefit (oracle ceiling). The per-type decomposition reveals that routing works best where perturbations are localized (visual: 66.8% of ceiling captured) and least where perturbations are distributed (spatial: 25.6%). This naturally leads to the mechanistic analysis section in the paper explaining which processing stages each perturbation type disrupts. For Claim 2, the headline is: \\\"Routing by perturbation type improves over universal LoRA by 6.0 ± 2.3% (p = 0.008), with gains concentrated on perturbation types that disrupt localized processing stages.\\\" This reframing adds nuance rather than claiming universal superiority.\\n\\n\\nThe ablation ladder shows a clean monotonic ordering (Frozen < Universal < Meta-Brain < Oracle) with all adjacent pairs statistically significant at p < 0.05. Step sizes decrease going up the ladder: +11.8 (baseline adaptation), +6.0 (routing benefit), +3.9 (routing quality gap). The largest step is Frozen → Universal, confirming that simply adding LoRA adaptation captures most of the low-hanging fruit. The Universal → Meta-Brain step (+6.0%) is the headline number for Claim 2 — routing by perturbation type provides meaningful additional benefit beyond uniform adaptation, but the advantage is moderate (not dominant). The diminishing step sizes reflect a fundamental pattern: each successive refinement addresses a smaller remaining error surface. Standard deviations are 1.8-2.4%, typical for LIBERO-Plus with per-seed task ordering variance. Meta-Brain's higher std (2.4%) reflects routing sensitivity to seed-specific perturbation distributions.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"FTM/FLA Reproduction — Visual-Only Baseline\",\n    \"description\": \"Reproduce FTM (4K params) and FLA (4.7M params) on pi0 for visual perturbation types. Compare with LoRA-V and Meta-Brain to establish visual-only baselines and demonstrate Meta-Brain's multi-type advantage.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"ftm_camera\": 69.8,\n      \"ftm_light\": 76.4,\n      \"ftm_background\": 72.6,\n      \"ftm_noise\": 68.2,\n      \"ftm_visual_avg\": 71.75,\n      \"ftm_overall\": 65.3,\n      \"fla_camera\": 73.8,\n      \"fla_light\": 80.2,\n      \"fla_background\": 76.8,\n      \"fla_noise\": 75.4,\n      \"fla_visual_avg\": 76.55,\n      \"fla_overall\": 68.1,\n      \"lora_v_visual_avg\": 78.3,\n      \"meta_brain_visual_avg\": 74.0,\n      \"meta_brain_overall\": 71.4,\n      \"meta_brain_advantage_over_ftm_overall\": 6.1,\n      \"meta_brain_advantage_over_fla_overall\": 3.3\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Visual Perturbation Methods Comparison (%)\",\n        \"header\": [\n          \"Method\",\n          \"Params\",\n          \"camera\",\n          \"light\",\n          \"bg\",\n          \"noise\",\n          \"Visual Avg\",\n          \"Overall\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"FTM\",\n            \"4K\",\n            69.8,\n            76.4,\n            72.6,\n            68.2,\n            71.75,\n            65.3\n          ],\n          [\n            \"FLA\",\n            \"4.7M\",\n            73.8,\n            80.2,\n            76.8,\n            75.4,\n            76.55,\n            68.1\n          ],\n          [\n            \"LoRA-V\",\n            \"~2M\",\n            75.2,\n            82.0,\n            78.4,\n            77.6,\n            78.3,\n            67.4\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Meta-Brain\",\n            \"~6M\",\n            71.0,\n            77.6,\n            74.4,\n            73.0,\n            74.0,\n            71.4\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Universal\",\n            \"~2M\",\n            62.8,\n            69.4,\n            66.2,\n            63.0,\n            65.35,\n            65.4\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"This experiment establishes two key points for the paper: (1) Visual-only methods are competitive on visual perturbations but fail as general robustness solutions — there is no free lunch. (2) Meta-Brain's routing approach provides the best overall coverage by handling all perturbation types, even though it doesn't maximize any single type. For Claim 3, FTM serves as an extreme point on the parameter-efficiency scatter plot: incredible visual-only efficiency but zero spatial/physical coverage. Meta-Brain occupies a qualitatively different position: moderate efficiency with universal coverage.\\n\\n\\nFTM at 4K params is remarkably parameter-efficient for visual perturbations (71.75% visual avg) but fundamentally cannot handle spatial/physical types, leaving those at frozen baseline levels and yielding only 65.3% overall. FLA at 4.7M params approaches LoRA-V quality on visual types (76.55% vs 78.3%) but also cannot generalize beyond visual. Meta-Brain's overall advantage (71.4% vs 65.3-68.1%) comes entirely from spatial and physical perturbation handling. On visual types alone, LoRA-V (78.3%) > FLA (76.55%) > Meta-Brain routed (74.0%) > FTM (71.75%), showing that specialized visual methods can match or exceed routing-based correction for visual perturbations specifically. The key message: FTM/FLA are excellent visual-only solutions but cannot serve as general robustness methods. Meta-Brain's value is breadth of coverage, not per-type optimality. FTM's per-parameter efficiency on visual types (11.7%/0.004M = 2925%/M) dwarfs all other methods but is limited to a single perturbation modality.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Parameter Efficiency Analysis\",\n    \"description\": \"Compute gain-over-frozen per method, normalize by trainable parameters. Produce the parameter efficiency scatter plot (x=params log scale, y=gain-over-baseline). Include Meta-Brain, Universal LoRA, FTM, FLA, VLA-GSE (from paper, different backbone caveat).\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"meta_brain_gain\": 17.8,\n      \"meta_brain_params_M\": 6,\n      \"meta_brain_gain_per_M\": 2.97,\n      \"universal_gain\": 11.8,\n      \"universal_params_M\": 2,\n      \"universal_gain_per_M\": 5.9,\n      \"ftm_gain\": 11.7,\n      \"ftm_params_M\": 0.004,\n      \"ftm_gain_per_M\": 2925.0,\n      \"fla_gain\": 14.5,\n      \"fla_params_M\": 4.7,\n      \"fla_gain_per_M\": 3.09,\n      \"vla_gse_gain\": 27.6,\n      \"vla_gse_params_M\": 114,\n      \"vla_gse_gain_per_M\": 0.24,\n      \"meta_brain_pct_of_vla_gse_gain\": 64.5,\n      \"meta_brain_pct_of_vla_gse_params\": 5.3\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Parameter Efficiency Comparison\",\n        \"header\": [\n          \"Method\",\n          \"Backbone\",\n          \"Params (M)\",\n          \"Gain over Frozen (%)\",\n          \"Gain/M Params\",\n          \"Coverage\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"FTM\",\n            \"pi0\",\n            0.004,\n            11.7,\n            2925.0,\n            \"Visual only\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Universal LoRA\",\n            \"pi0\",\n            2,\n            11.8,\n            5.9,\n            \"All types\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"FLA\",\n            \"pi0\",\n            4.7,\n            14.5,\n            3.09,\n            \"Visual only\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Meta-Brain\",\n            \"pi0\",\n            6,\n            17.8,\n            2.97,\n            \"All types\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"VLA-GSE†\",\n            \"Qwen3-VL-4B\",\n            114,\n            27.6,\n            0.24,\n            \"All types\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The scatter plot is the visual centerpiece of Claim 3. The key insight is that Meta-Brain extends the Pareto frontier established by Universal LoRA — it proves that routing-based specialization extracts more gain per parameter than simply increasing uniform LoRA rank. The framing should be \\\"complementary to capacity routing, not replacement\\\": Meta-Brain operates in a different region of the parameter-efficiency space than VLA-GSE. For resource-constrained deployment (edge devices, real-time control), Meta-Brain offers a practical operating point. An ideal system might compose both: Meta-Brain for type-aware correction + capacity routing for residual error.\\n\\n\\nMeta-Brain achieves 2.97%/M gain-per-parameter, sitting in the middle of the efficiency spectrum. Universal LoRA is the most efficient multi-type method (5.9%/M) but has a lower absolute gain ceiling. FTM is an extreme outlier (2925%/M) demonstrating that ultra-compact methods exist for single-type perturbations, but this efficiency doesn't scale to multi-type coverage. VLA-GSE achieves the highest absolute gain (27.6%) but at 0.24%/M efficiency — a 12× parameter overhead vs Meta-Brain for 55% more gain. The headline for Claim 3: \\\"Meta-Brain achieves 64.5% of VLA-GSE's robustness improvement using 5.3% of its trainable parameters, demonstrating perturbation-diagnostic routing as a parameter-efficient alternative to capacity-based expert routing.\\\" The different-backbone caveat (pi0 vs Qwen3-VL-4B) applies to absolute numbers but the efficiency ratio is still informative.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Correction Memory Ablation — With/Without CM\",\n    \"description\": \"Compare Meta-Brain with and without Correction Memory module. CM modulates routing weights using retrieved exemplars from a FAISS-indexed memory bank. Quantify marginal gain overall and per perturbation type.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"meta_brain_no_cm\": 71.4,\n      \"meta_brain_with_cm\": 72.6,\n      \"cm_ablation_delta\": 1.2,\n      \"cm_delta_visual\": 0.8,\n      \"cm_delta_spatial\": 0.4,\n      \"cm_delta_physical\": 2.8,\n      \"cm_alpha_learned\": 0.23,\n      \"cm_retrieval_latency_ms\": 1.4\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Correction Memory Ablation (%)\",\n        \"header\": [\n          \"Configuration\",\n          \"Visual\",\n          \"Spatial\",\n          \"Physical\",\n          \"Overall\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Meta-Brain (no CM)\",\n            74.0,\n            64.8,\n            74.2,\n            71.4\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Meta-Brain + CM\",\n            74.8,\n            65.2,\n            77.0,\n            72.6\n          ],\n          [\n            \"CM Delta\",\n            0.8,\n            0.4,\n            2.8,\n            1.2\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"CM's marginal contribution (1.2%) is expected in the median tier. It validates the module as a sound idea (positive delta on all types) but not a paper-defining contribution. Present CM as \\\"optional module, +1.2% overall, primarily benefiting recurrent physical perturbations.\\\" The per-type pattern adds a small mechanistic insight: exemplar-based routing modulation works best when perturbations are consistent (physics changes) rather than combinatorially varied (spatial layouts). For the paper, CM goes in the ablation section with 1-2 sentences, not a dedicated section.\\n\\n\\nCM provides a marginal +1.2% overall gain, concentrated on physical perturbations (+2.8%) where the same robot dynamics changes recur consistently across episodes — exemplar retrieval identifies \\\"I've seen this perturbation before\\\" and sharpens the routing weight toward LoRA-P. Visual gain is small (+0.8%) because the PAR router already routes visual perturbations confidently (diag 0.82) and CM's modulation has little room to improve. Spatial gain is minimal (+0.4%) because spatial perturbations are fundamentally different each time (different object positions) so retrieved exemplars provide weak signal. The learned alpha parameter (0.23) confirms the system's own assessment: CM contributes ~23% weighting to the final routing decision, indicating it's a secondary correction to PAR, not a primary signal. The 1.4ms retrieval latency adds negligible overhead to the 10Hz control loop. CM is worth including as an optional module but does not change the core story.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Router Architecture Comparison — MLP vs Linear vs Attention\",\n    \"description\": \"Compare PAR router variants on PaliGemma layer 6: (a) 2-layer MLP (default, ~50K params), (b) linear classifier (~6K params), (c) cross-attention router (~120K params). Evaluate both routing accuracy and downstream task success to test whether classification accuracy improvements translate to task performance.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"mlp_routing_accuracy\": 93.8,\n      \"mlp_task_success\": 71.4,\n      \"mlp_params\": 50000,\n      \"linear_routing_accuracy\": 90.6,\n      \"linear_task_success\": 70.2,\n      \"linear_params\": 6000,\n      \"attention_routing_accuracy\": 94.2,\n      \"attention_task_success\": 71.2,\n      \"attention_params\": 120000,\n      \"mlp_vs_linear_task_gap\": 1.2,\n      \"mlp_vs_attention_task_gap\": 0.2\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Router Architecture Comparison\",\n        \"header\": [\n          \"Router\",\n          \"Params\",\n          \"Routing Acc (%)\",\n          \"Task Success (%)\",\n          \"Inference Overhead (ms)\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Linear\",\n            \"6K\",\n            90.6,\n            70.2,\n            0.02\n          ],\n          [\n            \"MLP (default)\",\n            \"50K\",\n            93.8,\n            71.4,\n            0.05\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Cross-Attention\",\n            \"120K\",\n            94.2,\n            71.2,\n            0.18\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"This experiment addresses reviewer concern C1 (\\\"Phase 0 classification is too easy\\\") from a different angle: yes, the classification is relatively easy (even a linear classifier gets 90.6%), but the task-success gap between 90.6% and 93.8% routing accuracy is 1.2% — confirming that routing accuracy does matter at the margins. The MLP's 3.2% classification advantage over linear translates to 1.2% task success gain, roughly 1:0.37 ratio. Beyond 93.8%, additional classification gains have near-zero marginal task benefit. This helps calibrate the oracle-PAR gap analysis: the 3.9% task gap from oracle routing quality corresponds to moving from ~93.8% to ~100% classification accuracy, and the nonlinear mapping suggests diminishing returns as routing approaches perfection.\\n\\n\\nMLP (default) achieves 93.8% routing accuracy and 71.4% task success, offering the best cost/performance tradeoff. Linear router loses 3.2% routing accuracy, which translates to a modest 1.2% task success drop — the per-token representation at layer 6 is already quite separable (Phase 0 showed F1=0.993 with MLP), so a linear boundary captures most of the structure but misses some nonlinear decision boundaries near cluster edges. Attention router adds 0.4% routing accuracy over MLP (the classes are well-separated enough that attention over token sequences adds minimal information beyond mean pooling) but this doesn't translate to task success improvement (71.2% vs 71.4%) — the 0.4% classification gain falls within the noise floor of rollout evaluation. The attention router's 2.4× parameter overhead is unjustified. Conclusion: MLP is the right choice, confirming the Phase 0 finding that perturbation type is a simple classification problem at layer 6.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"6-Class vs 3-Class Routing Comparison\",\n    \"description\": \"Compare fine-grained 6-class routing (camera/light/background/noise/layout/robot, 6 LoRA experts ~12M params) with coarse 3-class routing (Visual/Spatial/Physical, 3 experts ~6M). Tests whether finer-grained perturbation-type routing justifies additional parameters and architectural complexity.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"three_class_overall\": 71.4,\n      \"three_class_routing_accuracy\": 93.8,\n      \"three_class_params_M\": 6,\n      \"six_class_overall\": 72.2,\n      \"six_class_routing_accuracy\": 89.4,\n      \"six_class_params_M\": 12,\n      \"delta_overall\": 0.8,\n      \"six_class_camera\": 72.8,\n      \"six_class_light\": 79.2,\n      \"six_class_background\": 76.0,\n      \"six_class_noise\": 74.6,\n      \"six_class_distractor\": 66.0,\n      \"six_class_layout\": 65.2,\n      \"six_class_robot\": 81.6\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"6-Class vs 3-Class Comparison (%)\",\n        \"header\": [\n          \"Configuration\",\n          \"Classes\",\n          \"Params\",\n          \"Routing Acc\",\n          \"Overall\",\n          \"Δ vs 3-class\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"3-class Meta-Brain\",\n            3,\n            \"~6M\",\n            93.8,\n            71.4,\n            \"—\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"6-class Meta-Brain\",\n            6,\n            \"~12M\",\n            89.4,\n            72.2,\n            0.8\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"6-Class Per-Type Breakdown vs 3-Class (%)\",\n        \"header\": [\n          \"Perturbation\",\n          \"3-class\",\n          \"6-class\",\n          \"Delta\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"camera\",\n            71.0,\n            72.8,\n            1.8\n          ],\n          [\n            \"light\",\n            77.6,\n            79.2,\n            1.6\n          ],\n          [\n            \"background\",\n            74.4,\n            76.0,\n            1.6\n          ],\n          [\n            \"noise\",\n            73.0,\n            74.6,\n            1.6\n          ],\n          [\n            \"distractor\",\n            65.2,\n            66.0,\n            0.8\n          ],\n          [\n            \"layout\",\n            64.4,\n            65.2,\n            0.8\n          ],\n          [\n            \"robot\",\n            74.2,\n            81.6,\n            7.4\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"This directly addresses reviewer concern C5 (\\\"3-class taxonomy may be too coarse\\\"). The answer is: 3-class is nearly optimal for this architecture. The 6-class robot improvement (+7.4%) is notable in isolation but doesn't offset the systemic costs of finer routing. The Phase 0 intra-Visual similarity finding (6-class accuracy 99.82% but intra-Visual confusion is near-zero) predicted this outcome: types that cluster together in activation space don't need separate experts. Include this as a 1-paragraph ablation result: \\\"Finer 6-class routing adds +0.8% at 2× parameters, confirming that the 3-class taxonomy aligns with the mechanistic distinction in VLA processing stages.\\\"\\n\\n\\n6-class routing provides only +0.8% overall improvement over 3-class while doubling the parameter budget (12M vs 6M) and dropping routing accuracy by 4.4% (89.4% vs 93.8%). The marginal gain arises primarily from the robot expert (+3.8% from dedicated robot LoRA vs shared Physical LoRA) where the 1-type Physical class benefits from fully dedicated parameters. Intra-Visual perturbation types (camera/light/background/noise) do NOT benefit significantly from separate experts because they share the same failure mode (early representation corruption) — Phase 0 6-class analysis already showed high intra-Visual similarity. The spatial split (distractor/layout) gains marginally because both types require trajectory-level reasoning that LoRA cannot provide. The 4.4% routing accuracy drop with 6 classes partially offsets any per-expert specialization gain: more classes means more routing errors, and each error routes to a more narrowly specialized (thus more harmful when mismatched) expert. Net assessment: 3-class is the better design point.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Cross-Backbone Validation — SmolVLA\",\n    \"description\": \"Replicate Phase 0 classification + Phase 1 routing pipeline on SmolVLA to test whether perturbation-diagnostic routing generalizes beyond pi0. SmolVLA uses a different vision encoder (SigLIP) and language backbone (SmolLM), testing architecture independence.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"smolvla_phase0_3class_f1\": 0.938,\n      \"smolvla_phase0_best_layer\": 4,\n      \"smolvla_frozen_baseline\": 46.2,\n      \"smolvla_universal_lora\": 56.8,\n      \"smolvla_meta_brain\": 62.4,\n      \"smolvla_gain_over_frozen\": 16.2,\n      \"smolvla_gain_over_universal\": 5.6,\n      \"smolvla_routing_diagonality\": 0.73,\n      \"smolvla_visual_specialization_delta\": 10.4,\n      \"smolvla_spatial_specialization_delta\": 2.8,\n      \"smolvla_physical_specialization_delta\": 7.6\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Cross-Backbone Comparison (pi0 vs SmolVLA)\",\n        \"header\": [\n          \"Metric\",\n          \"pi0\",\n          \"SmolVLA\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Phase 0 3-class F1\",\n            0.993,\n            0.938\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Best classification layer\",\n            6,\n            4\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Frozen baseline (%)\",\n            53.6,\n            46.2\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Universal LoRA (%)\",\n            65.4,\n            56.8\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Meta-Brain (%)\",\n            71.4,\n            62.4\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Gain over frozen (%)\",\n            17.8,\n            16.2\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Gain over universal (%)\",\n            6.0,\n            5.6\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Routing diagonality\",\n            0.77,\n            0.73\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Visual specialization delta (%)\",\n            12.95,\n            10.4\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Spatial specialization delta (%)\",\n            4.3,\n            2.8\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"This experiment addresses reviewer concern C3 (\\\"single backbone, limited generalizability\\\"). The result is clearly positive: the qualitative pattern replicates exactly (same per-type specialization ranking, same ablation ordering, comparable relative gains). The Phase 0 F1 drop (0.993 → 0.938) is expected — SigLIP and PaliGemma have different feature structures — but 0.938 is still high enough for effective routing. The spatial underperformance replication across backbones strengthens the mechanistic interpretation: it's a property of the perturbation type, not the backbone. For the paper, include as a validation subsection: \\\"Cross-backbone results on SmolVLA replicate the qualitative findings, with comparable routing gains (+5.6% vs +6.0% over universal), confirming that perturbation-diagnostic routing is an architecture-independent principle.\\\" This is a P2 experiment — include if time/GPU budget permits, otherwise cite the Phase 0 cross-backbone evidence from SAFE/probing literature.\\n\\n\\nSmolVLA confirms the core finding: frozen VLA activations encode perturbation type (F1=0.938, lower than pi0's 0.993 due to SigLIP's different feature structure but still high). The best classification layer shifts to layer 4 (SmolVLA has fewer layers than PaliGemma). Meta-Brain on SmolVLA achieves +16.2% gain over frozen (comparable to pi0's +17.8%) and +5.6% over universal (comparable to pi0's +6.0%), demonstrating that the routing benefit is architecture-independent. Absolute numbers are lower (62.4% vs 71.4%) due to SmolVLA's weaker backbone capacity (SmolLM-360M vs pi0's larger model). The per-type specialization pattern replicates: visual shows the largest delta (10.4%), physical moderate (7.6%), spatial weak (2.8%). Spatial underperformance persists across backbones, confirming it's a fundamental limitation of single-step LoRA correction for trajectory-level perturbations, not an artifact of pi0's architecture. Routing diagonality (0.73) is slightly lower than pi0 (0.77), reflecting SigLIP's less structured intermediate representations for perturbation classification.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"LoRA Layer Placement Ablation\",\n    \"description\": \"Vary which PaliGemma layers receive LoRA: (a) early (2-6), (b) mid (6-12), (c) late (12-16), (d) all layers. Test hypothesis that perturbation types optimally correct at different processing stages.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"lora_v_early\": 80.2,\n      \"lora_v_mid\": 74.8,\n      \"lora_v_late\": 71.4,\n      \"lora_v_all\": 78.3,\n      \"lora_s_early\": 64.6,\n      \"lora_s_mid\": 70.2,\n      \"lora_s_late\": 66.8,\n      \"lora_s_all\": 68.0,\n      \"lora_p_early\": 72.8,\n      \"lora_p_mid\": 75.2,\n      \"lora_p_late\": 80.6,\n      \"lora_p_all\": 78.0,\n      \"optimal_placement_v\": \"early (2-6)\",\n      \"optimal_placement_s\": \"mid (6-12)\",\n      \"optimal_placement_p\": \"late (12-16)\",\n      \"optimal_combined_overall\": 73.8,\n      \"all_layer_combined_overall\": 71.4,\n      \"layer_placement_gain\": 2.4\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Layer Placement × Expert Type (own-type success rate %)\",\n        \"header\": [\n          \"Layer Range\",\n          \"LoRA-V (Visual)\",\n          \"LoRA-S (Spatial)\",\n          \"LoRA-P (Physical)\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Early (2-6)\",\n            80.2,\n            64.6,\n            72.8\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Mid (6-12)\",\n            74.8,\n            70.2,\n            75.2\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Late (12-16)\",\n            71.4,\n            66.8,\n            80.6\n          ],\n          [\n            \"All layers\",\n            78.3,\n            68.0,\n            78.0\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Optimal Layer Placement Summary\",\n        \"header\": [\n          \"Expert\",\n          \"Best Layers\",\n          \"Own-Type Success\",\n          \"Δ vs All-Layer\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"LoRA-V\",\n            \"Early (2-6)\",\n            80.2,\n            1.9\n          ],\n          [\n            \"LoRA-S\",\n            \"Mid (6-12)\",\n            70.2,\n            2.2\n          ],\n          [\n            \"LoRA-P\",\n            \"Late (12-16)\",\n            80.6,\n            2.6\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"This is a P2 experiment that adds mechanistic depth but is not essential for the core claims. The crossed peak pattern (V-early, S-mid, P-late) aligns with how VLA processing stages work: visual encoding → spatial reasoning → action prediction. This provides evidence for the paper's framing that perturbation types disrupt different processing stages. The +2.4% gain from optimal layer placement is small but suggests a natural extension: layer-aware routing that not only selects the right expert but applies it at the right layers. This could close part of the oracle-PAR gap (3.9%) without improving classification accuracy. For the paper, include as a supplementary analysis with the crossed-peak figure — it's visually compelling and mechanistically interpretive. Do not pursue layer-specific routing as a primary contribution; it's a natural future work direction.\\n\\nThe layer placement results support the mechanistic hypothesis with moderate effect sizes. LoRA-V benefits most from early layers (+1.9% over all-layer) because visual perturbations (camera/light/background/noise) corrupt the initial visual encoding in PaliGemma's early layers — correcting at the source prevents error propagation. LoRA-S benefits from mid layers (+2.2% over all-layer) because spatial reasoning (object relationships, layout understanding) is computed in middle transformer layers where multi-head attention integrates spatial context — though the absolute improvement remains modest due to the fundamental trajectory-level limitation. LoRA-P benefits from late layers (+2.6% over all-layer) because physical perturbations manifest at the action prediction stage in late layers where dynamics models are implicitly computed. All-layer placement performs as a reasonable default (second-best for each expert type) because the LoRA rank is distributed across layers that contribute varying amounts. The combined optimal placement (type-specific layers per expert) yields 73.8% overall (+2.4% over uniform all-layer 71.4%), suggesting layer-aware routing is a promising extension.\\n\"\n  }\n]","numLines":1067,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1067}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01GRQTAwoWPfPzrds1JH1Mqq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have all the data I need. Let me compose a complete LaTeX paper. Let me organize the key data points:\n\n**Experiment 1: LoRA Expert Training**\n- Cross-type matrix showing specialized vs universal performance\n- Key: Visual expert +13% over universal on visual, Spatial expert only +4.3%, Physical +9%\n\n**Experiment 2: Meta-Brain Assembly**\n- Overall: 71.4% (vs 65.4% universal, 53.6% frozen)\n- Per-type breakdown with routing weight matrix\n\n**Experiment 3: Oracle Routing**\n- Oracle: 75.3%, gap to Meta-Brain: 3.9%\n\n\n- The ablation ladder shows clear progression from frozen baseline through universal to Meta-Brain to oracle, with each step statistically significant. Meta-Brain also outperforms both FTM and FLA baselines on overall performance despite their stronger visual-specific results. Finally, Meta-Brain achieves substantial parameter efficiency, gaining 17.8% improvement with only 6M additional parameters compared to the baseline.\n\nNow looking at the remaining experiments: the correction memory component adds 1.2% overall with stronger gains in physical reasoning, the MLP router performs best among the three architectures tested, and the 6-class formulation slightly edges out 3-class despite doubling parameters. Cross-backbone evaluation on SmolVLA shows the approach generalizes well with a 5.6% gain, and I'm examining how layer placement affects performance.\n\nThe layer placement ablation reveals that distributing vision early, spatial mid, and physical late yields 73.8% compared to 71.4% when applied uniformly across all layers. Now I'm planning the paper structure, organizing it around the core method, then moving through experiments that establish expert specialization, present the main results with ablations, compare against visual-only baselines, analyze parameter efficiency, and dive into mechanistic insights about routing and layer placement before wrapping up the remaining ablations.\n\nWriting the LaTeX...","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2636,"cache_creation_input_tokens":15469,"cache_read_input_tokens":23112,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":15469},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f10e81d9-0851-41df-87e8-bf73f3c49003","uuid":"fc83018d-caa8-4882-9d8d-207ca82e3a6e"}
